window.DOC_SEARCH=[{"title":"API reference","url":"api.html","text":" API reference Choose a module to browse signatures, parameters, return values, and source code. API modules Module Contents graph_tiger.graphs Graph generators, dataset loaders, and small reference networks. graph_tiger.measures Structural measures and measure-name dispatch. graph_tiger.attacks Node and edge selection and attack simulation. graph_tiger.defenses Node protection, edge addition, and rewiring. graph_tiger.simulations Shared execution, random state, and plotting. graph_tiger.cascading Global rerouting and local redistribution cascades. graph_tiger.diffusion SIS and SIR epidemic processes and interventions. graph_tiger.influence Independent-cascade, threshold, voter, and competitive influence processes. graph_tiger.utils Sparse graph matrices and spectral utilities. "},{"title":"Attack types","url":"attacks.html","text":" Guide Attack types On this page Node removal Edge removal Report Initial and recalculated policies Follow damage on a water network Compare node and edge attacks on KY-2 Other selection policies State the threatened component, removal budget, attacker information, whether scores are recalculated, and the service outcome. Compare targeted policies with random removal under the same budget. Node removal id_node rd_node ib_node rb_node rnd_node Edge removal id_edge rd_edge ib_edge rb_edge rnd_edge Report Topology, attack order, retained outcome curve, random-reference uncertainty, and exact budget. Initial and recalculated policies Initial degree (ID) ranks nodes once by incident-edge count. Initial betweenness (IB) ranks them by shortest-path brokerage. Recalculated degree (RD) and betweenness (RB) update their scores after every removal. Recalculation can adapt to changed structure, at added computational cost; it does not guarantee a stronger attack on every graph. Follow damage on a water network The KY-2 water-distribution graph contains 809 junctions connected by pipe links. We remove 0, 10, and 30 junctions using recalculated degree: after each removal, the next target is chosen from the remaining graph. These snapshots ask where damage occurs and which surviving junctions can still reach one another; they do not model hydraulic flow, pressure, or delivered water demand. The code reads the dataset’s node positions once, computes the attack order once, and removes progressively longer prefixes from fresh graph copies. It then identifies the largest connected component in each copy. Teal marks that component, amber marks other surviving components, and red crosses mark removed junctions. Pale original links provide context; darker links belong to the surviving graph. Keeping positions fixed lets you follow the same junction across all three views. Draw the water-network snapshots — code The same network coordinates are used in all three states. Teal denotes the largest surviving component, amber other components, and red crosses removed nodes. Faint edges retain the original network as context. These snapshots use RD, not the RB attack used in the repair study. Run data (CSV) · Parameters and versions · Complete experiment script . Select a plot to open it at full size. The largest component contains 809, 797, 772 junctions after 0, 10, 30 removals, respectively. Read those counts together with the maps, not just the amount of colored ink. A removed junction can separate nearby branches even when most of the drawing still looks intact. These maps use recalculated degree, whereas the repair experiment uses recalculated betweenness; they illustrate different attack scenarios. Shared plotting and export options . Compare node and edge attacks on KY-2 Both panels start from the same 809-node KY-2 water-distribution graph. Each trial removes up to 30 nodes or 30 edges using random, initial-degree, recalculated-degree, initial-betweenness, or recalculated-betweenness selection. The outcome is the largest surviving component divided by 809: the share of original junctions still connected within one component. The code loops over policies and ten seeds, creates a fresh simulation for each trial, and normalizes its component-size history before computing the mean and 10th–90th percentiles. Betweenness is estimated using 80 sampled sources. Read node attacks and edge attacks separately: equal removal counts do not represent equal damage or cost. Run the attack comparison — code KY-2, 30 removals, seeds 0–9. Lines are means; shading is the 10th–90th percentile across runs, not a confidence interval. Betweenness uses 80 sampled sources. Compare strategies within a panel: one node removal is not the same intervention as one edge removal. Run data (CSV) · Parameters and versions · Complete experiment script . Select a plot to open it at full size. A steep drop means a removal separates a large part of the network, rather than merely deleting one more component. Comparing initial and recalculated policies tests whether updating the ranking as damage accumulates matters for this graph. Recalculated betweenness ends at 31.4% mean retained connectivity for node attacks and 55.6% for edge attacks. These are separate removal budgets, not equal-cost damage. The shaded region is the 10th–90th percentile range across runs, not a confidence interval. Exact deterministic policies may produce identical runs; approximation and random removal introduce variation. Draw the network and save attack snapshots → Other selection policies For edge ID and RD, TIGER scores an edge {u,v} by degree(u) * degree(v) . RD updates this score after each planned deletion. IB/RB rank shortest-path betweenness, with approx counting sampled source nodes even for edge attacks. Reference Family Selection idea What it does not establish NetShield (ns_node) Greedily selects a set using a leading-eigenpair approximation to spectral-radius reduction. Reference Not an optimal LCC attack or defense for every process. PageRank / eigenvector Ranks recursively central nodes; emphasizes well-connected neighborhoods. Reference Not a direct measure of geographic exposure or operational demand. Line-graph edge policies Treats each original edge as a node and ranks the resulting line graph. Can be expensive around hubs; differs from endpoint-degree-product scoring. All supported keys and selection functions . In Attack, recalculated attack orders are generated on a working copy before node protection is applied. For an attacker that retargets a defended network, write that decision loop explicitly and state the changed threat model. "},{"title":"Cascading failures","url":"cascades.html","text":" Guide Select a cascading-failure mechanism On this page Local redistribution Choose a local allocation policy What spare capacity means Coordinating a whole round with maximum flow Supply application loads and capacities One failed source: headroom and overflow Two failed sources: why coordination matters Watch failures propagate Compare beta on a power network State and timing Motter–Lai: reroute, then remove overloads Crucitti: reroute through changing edge efficiencies Compare routing models on a power-grid subnetwork Continue to completed cascade outcomes A cascade begins when an initial failure changes the work carried by the remaining network. Choose the model by how that work moves and what happens when capacity is exceeded. The examples below use an illustrative load-sharing chain and an empirical power-network topology—not a social network. These are simplified load and routing models, not electrical power-flow simulations. Model Load Overload Outcome Motter–Lai (2002) Global shortest-path betweenness Node fails Retained connectivity Crucitti–Latora–Marchiori (2004) Most-efficient weighted paths Incident edge efficiency degrades Weighted network efficiency Local load sharing: Wei et al. (2012) Failed-node load moves to functioning neighbors Over-capacity recipient fails next round Connectivity, failed nodes, and lost service Beta intuition. A functioning neighbor receives weight proportional to degree**beta . At beta=0 , neighbors receive equal shares. Increasing beta directs more load toward high-degree neighbors, which may absorb load or become concentrated overload points. Local redistribution By default, L i (0) = k i , where k i is the intact degree, and capacity C i = (1+r)L i (0). When i fails, its current load is shared among functioning neighbors N i + : ΔL i → j = L i k j β ∑ u ∈ N i + k u β Degrees in the weights come from the intact graph. Transfers from the same round are accumulated before checking new overloads. If two recipients have degrees 1 and 3 and the failed load is 8, β=0 gives shares 4 and 4; β=1 gives 2 and 6; β=2 gives 0.8 and 7.2. Choose a local allocation policy All four policies move the full workload of each newly failed node to functioning neighbors. They differ in which neighbors receive it, not whether excess work can be discarded. Overloads are checked only after all transfers in the round have been accumulated. Work becomes lost_load only when its source has no eligible recipient. allocation Where displaced work goes degree Default. Intact-degree weights raised to beta; beta=0 gives equal sharing. greedy Fill the largest spare capacities first, independently for each failed source; split any remainder equally among its recipients. proportional Divide the full workload in proportion to spare capacity; use equal shares when all spare capacities are zero. max_flow Coordinate all failed sources to maximize the work placed within shared spare capacities, then split each source’s remainder equally among its recipients. What spare capacity means For a recipient j, C j is its fixed capacity and L j its load before this round. Its headroom is s j =max(0, C j −L j ). For one failed source, d is its displaced load, q its number of eligible neighbors, and S the sum of their headrooms. s j = max ( 0 , C j − L j ) , S = ∑ j s j Proportional allocation assigns x j =d s j /S when S>0, and d/q otherwise. Greedy allocation visits neighbors in decreasing headroom, assigns the smaller of remaining work and headroom, then adds an equal share of any remainder to every eligible neighbor. Each source uses the same pre-round loads; greedy does not reserve capacity for other sources. Ties follow the original graph’s node insertion order. Coordinating a whole round with maximum flow The auxiliary flow network has source-to-failed-node arcs of capacity d i , failed-node-to-eligible-recipient arcs of capacity d i , and one recipient-to-sink arc of capacity s j per recipient. That single shared sink arc prevents different failures from spending the same headroom twice. TIGER uses NetworkX’s Edmonds–Karp solver with nodes and transfer arcs inserted in original graph node order. If f ij is the first-pass flow, the remaining work from source i is u i =d i −∑ j f ij . Its complete transfer is f ij +u i /q i . Overflow is still assigned even when it causes failure. This maximizes the capacity-fitting first pass, not long-term survival; tied maximum flows can lead to different later cascades. Record graph insertion order and NetworkX version when reproducing ties. Supply application loads and capacities initial_load and capacities are complete node-to-value dictionaries of finite nonnegative numbers. Omit loads to use intact unweighted degrees; omit capacities to use (1+r) times the supplied or default initial loads. Explicit capacities are used directly, without multiplying by (1+r) again. Inputs are copied and restored on reset. Existing node-defense options can still increase those capacities. initial_failures supplies an exact initiating set instead of attack selection; an empty list means no initiating failures. This option and the supplied load/capacity mappings apply to local_load_sharing only. The field last_transfers[(i, j)] reports the completed transfers of the latest redistribution round. lost_load is cumulative unserved work; shed_load remains a backward-compatible alias, including in older CSVs and figures. Neither is a deliberate shedding control. One failed source: headroom and overflow A failed node F hands work to recipients a, b, and c, initially carrying zero work and having capacities 3, 5, and 7. Run the same four policies with 12 and then 18 units displaced. Equal sharing uses beta=0. The dashed marks show available headroom; a bar above its mark means the recipient overloads in this round. Run the single-source comparison — code 12 units: enough total headroom, but equal sharing overloads a · PDF 18 units: all policies assign the full load, despite insufficient headroom · PDF With 12 units, greedy gives (0,5,7) and proportional gives (2.4,4,5.6); both fit. Equal sharing gives (4,4,4), overloading a despite sufficient total headroom. The fixed-order maximum-flow solution gives (3,5,4); it is another feasible allocation, not the uniquely best one. With 18 units, greedy and maximum flow give (4,6,8), while proportional gives (3.6,6,8.4). All recipients overload for those three policies. Equal sharing gives (6,6,6), initially overloading only a and b—showing that maximizing the first-pass fit does not necessarily minimize failures. Two failed sources: why coordination matters A has 5 units and can use U or V. B has 7 units and can use only U. The recipients start empty, with capacities 7 and 5. Arrows show permitted handoffs; their labels are the work actually transferred in one round. Gray nodes failed initially, orange indicates a new overload, and teal means functioning. All panels use the same positions, capacities, and initial failures. Run the shared-recipient comparison — code Equal sharing · PDF Greedy headroom allocation · PDF Proportional headroom allocation · PDF Coordinated maximum flow · PDF Greedy sends A’s 5 units to U, then B also sends its 7 units to U: U receives 12 against capacity 7 and fails. Proportional and equal sharing also overload U here. Maximum flow instead sends A’s 5 units to V and B’s 7 units to U, so neither recipient fails. No policy loses or discards work during this round. If B instead carries 9 units, even maximum flow must send U two units beyond its headroom; U then fails. Download the complete runner , transfer data , and settings and figure hashes . These small examples isolate allocation mechanics; they do not establish which policy is best for every application. Watch failures propagate This four-node chain isolates a single mechanism: a failed node hands its entire workload to its functioning neighbor. It is an invented example with arbitrary work units, not measured grid demand. F fails initially. A and B cannot absorb the incoming work; C has enough capacity to stop the cascade. The initial loads are (6, 2, 2, 1) and capacities are (6, 5, 8, 12) for F, A, B, C. Each snapshot follows one redistribution round. Pink nodes stay failed; their remaining workload is transferred in the next round. Lines show the original connections, not functioning service. Run the chain example — code Read downward: F sends 6 to A; A sends 8 to B; B sends 10 to C. C finishes with 11, below capacity 12. All 11 initial units are accounted for, and no workload is lost. The failed-node counts are 1, 2, 3, and 3. A stable count alone is not a stopping rule: B still has work to transfer after round 2. The cascade is complete after round 3, when every failed workload has been processed and no new overload remains. Download the states. Compare beta on a power network The electrical-network dataset contains 4,941 nodes and 6,594 edges. We treat it as a topology: initial load is degree, not measured power demand, and capacity is 1.2 times initial load. The highest-degree node fails first. Only the redistribution preference β changes between the three runs. The code follows 20 redistribution rounds and reads cumulative failed nodes and lost load separately. β=0 shares equally among functioning neighbors; β=1 or 2 increasingly favors neighbors with higher intact degree. Lost load is work with no functioning recipient, not another count of failed nodes. Run the local-sharing comparison — code Electrical-network dataset; one initial high-degree failure; r=0.2, β=0,1,2, seed 7. Initial load equals intact degree, not measured electrical demand. Failed nodes and shed load are distinct outcomes. Shifting load toward hubs need not reduce either outcome. Run data (CSV) · Parameters and versions · Complete experiment script . Select a plot to open it at full size. A flatter failure curve over this horizon indicates slower damage growth, not necessarily a smaller completed cascade. The longer-run section below checks the final outcome; choosing β from the first 20 rounds alone can be misleading. By round 20, β=0, 1 and 2 have caused 3,516, 3,714, 3,459 failures. These curves are still rising; see the completed-cascade comparison below. Local-sharing overloads satisfy L i >C i . Failed sources are processed once; a source with no functioning recipient contributes its current load to cumulative lost load. Fixed intact degrees are used even after damage. Continue until no unprocessed failure remains and no new overload appears. Reference State and timing Index 0 records the attacked state; indices 1 through steps record transitions. failed counts cumulative failures, status stores node loads in original node order, and lost_load records work that could not be passed to a functioning neighbor. Crucitti also records edge_efficiency and overloaded . Use run_single_sim() to inspect that realization’s history immediately. run_simulation() averages output and resets state after every run, including the last. Motter–Lai: reroute, then remove overloads Let F(t) be the cumulative failed-node set and G(t) the graph induced by functioning nodes. Set initial load to unnormalized node betweenness B i (G), excluding endpoints; split a shortest-path contribution among ties. Fix capacity before the attack: L i (0)= B i (G), C i =(1+r) L i (0) For each functioning node, B i (G(t)) = ∑ s<u; s,u≠i σ su (i)/σ su , where σ su counts shortest paths and σ su (i) counts those through i. Count each unordered pair once; unreachable pairs contribute zero. After the initial attack, recompute all loads from the surviving topology. Every node whose new load exceeds its fixed capacity fails in the same round: L i (t)= B i (G(t)) F (t+1)=F(t)∪{i∉F(t): L i (t)> C i } Stop when a round adds no failures. Disconnected source–target pairs carry no path contribution. A node with zero initial betweenness has zero base capacity in this implementation. Reference Crucitti: reroute through changing edge efficiencies Use the same initial betweenness loads and fixed capacities, but keep overloaded nodes functioning. Each initial edge has efficiency e ij (0)=1 and routing distance 1/e ij . At each round, calculate an endpoint factor: a i (t)= { 1 if L i (t)≤ C i C i L i (t) otherwise e ij (t+1)=min( a i (t), a j (t)) Recompute betweenness on shortest routes under the new reciprocal-efficiency distances. This is an assignment from the current capacity-to-load factors, not multiplication by last round’s efficiency. An edge can therefore recover as overload recedes. If both endpoints overload, the lower factor controls their undirected edge. E (t)= 1 n (t)(n(t)−1) ∑ i ≠ j 1 d i j Here d ij is the shortest sum of reciprocal edge efficiencies, n(t) counts functioning nodes, and unreachable pairs contribute zero. n(t)≤1 gives zero efficiency. TIGER stops when successive edge efficiencies are close under NumPy’s default isclose tolerance (rtol=10 −5 , atol=10 −8 ), or when the step limit is reached. Report both; oscillation can persist. Reference Compare routing models on a power-grid subnetwork Use the two-hop neighborhood of the highest-degree node in TIGER’s electrical-network dataset. This fixed selection gives 45 nodes and 71 connections. We use a small subnetwork so exact repeated routing calculations and its drawing remain easy to inspect. It is an induced topology: connections to the rest of the grid are excluded, so its results do not describe the full grid. Nodes represent grid components and edges represent connections in the source topology. The data used here supplies neither operating demand nor line limits. Loads are shortest-path betweenness, all initial edge weights are one, and the spring layout is not a geographic map. The larger local-sharing studies on this page use the full 4,941-node network. The same subnetwork and initial high-degree attack are used in all six runs. Node 2553 is attacked; the neighborhood was selected around node 2553. Set capacity to (1+r) times intact betweenness and compare r=0.2, 0.5, and 1 within each model. Motter–Lai removes overloaded nodes; report the largest component divided by the original 45 nodes. Crucitti–Latora–Marchiori retains overloaded nodes but changes edge efficiencies; report weighted efficiency divided by its intact value. The code applies the same initial attack and 200-round horizon to all six runs. It records retained connectivity for Motter–Lai and relative weighted efficiency for Crucitti. TIGER averages the latter over surviving nodes, so its denominator changes after the initial attack. Run the routing-model comparison — code Exact routing calculations, one initial high-degree removal, 200 rounds, seed 7. These deterministic parameter comparisons have no uncertainty band. Step zero is already attacked. At round 200, Motter–Lai retains 22.2%, 22.2%, 24.4% of original-node connectivity for r=0.2, 0.5, and 1. The corresponding Crucitti efficiency ratios are 24.0%, 25.0%, 26.5%. Compare capacities within a model, not percentages between these two different outcomes. The last 20 Crucitti rounds span efficiency-ratio ranges of 0.0067, 0.0040, 0.0000. A nonzero range indicates continuing variation: a plotted endpoint is not automatically a steady state. More capacity does not by itself guarantee a better finite-time routing outcome. Download all trajectories , network selection and settings , or the complete figure runner . Continue to completed cascade outcomes Return to exactly the electrical graph, initial failure, capacities, and β values used in the 20-round local-sharing experiment. Extend the run limit to 200 rounds rather than changing the model or selecting a different attack. The code retains both failed-node and lost-load histories and checks whether any failed node remains to be processed. Completion requires an empty pending-failure queue and stable totals. The plots show the active portion of the runs; the downloadable CSV also retains their flat tails. Run the cascades to completion — code Same electrical graph, initial degree attack, r=0.2 and seed 7 as the short run. Curves are displayed through stabilization; flat tails are retained in the CSV. Data (CSV) · Parameters · Rerun this study . Use the eventual totals to compare completed damage and the approach to those totals to compare timing. Two rules can produce different early trajectories yet fail the same number of nodes in the end. Completed failures for β=0, 1 and 2 are 4,941, 4,941, 4,801, respectively. The last observed changes occur at rounds 34, 33, 36. Equal sharing and β=1 ultimately fail the entire network; β=2 leaves 140 nodes functioning. This conclusion differs from comparing only the first 20 rounds. "},{"title":"Defense measures","url":"defenses.html","text":" Guide Defense measures On this page Edge policies Reconnect KY-2 after a fixed attack Node protection Improve topology before damage See the defense itself Hold the attack and outcome fixed when comparing node hardening, edge addition, or rewiring. Match both action count and meaningful cost whenever possible. Intervention Question Required baseline Protect nodes Which components receive hardening or immunity? Random protection with the same count. Add edges Where can feasible new routes create alternatives? Random feasible additions with matched cost. Rewire edges Can placement improve with fixed edge count? Random rewiring under the same feasibility rules. Edge policies Random addition samples nonedges. Preferential addition joins low-degree endpoints. Random-edge rewiring replaces a sampled edge; random-neighbor rewiring samples uniformly from oriented vertex–neighbor pairs for deletion (not a uniform vertex followed by a uniform neighbor). Preferential rewiring detaches a neighbor of a highest-degree node, while preferential-random rewiring detaches the higher-degree endpoint of a sampled edge. These are topological rules; the implementation does not enforce geographic cost or construction constraints. These policy families follow the edge-modification literature; operational feasibility remains study-specific. Reference Reference Reconnect KY-2 after a fixed attack First remove 30 KY-2 junctions using one recalculated-betweenness attack order (seed 17). Every repair trial starts from that same damaged graph. The experiment asks whether 30 edge additions or replacements can reconnect the surviving junctions, without restoring the removed ones. The code separates the common attack from the defense loop, runs ten seeds per repair policy, and divides each largest-component count by the original 809 nodes. Additions increase edge count; rewiring replaces one edge at a time. The dashed no-repair baseline makes the gain over the damaged state visible. Run the post-attack repair comparison — code Every policy starts from the same KY-2 graph after 30 RB node removals (attack seed 17). Ten defense seeds, 0–9; mean and 10th–90th percentile range. The dashed line is no repair. One addition increases edge count; one rewiring replaces an edge. These are action budgets, not equal construction costs. Run data (CSV) · Parameters and versions · Complete experiment script . Select a plot to open it at full size. An upward jump means a new connection has joined previously separate components. A flat segment may still change paths within a component, but that change is invisible to the selected connectivity measure. The plot evaluates topological reconnection, not geographic feasibility or engineering cost. The common damaged baseline retains 26.7% of original nodes. Low-degree addition reaches 96.3% after 30 repairs. Edge repair cannot restore the 30 removed nodes; geographic feasibility is not enforced. Edge count and engineering cost are different budgets. Added edges increase available infrastructure; rewiring keeps edge count fixed. Report both achieved connectivity and intervention cost before recommending a policy. Node protection In an Attack simulation, protected nodes survive selected removals. In a cascade, node defense doubles selected capacities; it does not immunize an initially attacked node. In epidemic simulations, node selection can confer immunity. Use the interpretation corresponding to the process. On the 34-node karate graph, compare no protection with protection of three randomly selected or NetShield-selected nodes. Each run then makes eight recalculated-degree attack attempts. Protection here means a selected node survives an attempted removal; it is not extra cascade capacity or epidemic recovery. The code uses an Attack simulation because the intervention prevents node deletion during an attack. TIGER computes the order before applying protection, so a protected target is skipped rather than replaced with another target. Twenty seeds provide the run distribution; component sizes are normalized by the original 34 nodes. Run the node-protection comparison — code Karate club; three protected nodes; seeds 0–19. A protected target survives an attempt, so the horizontal axis counts attempts, not successful removals. TIGER generates the RD attack order before applying protection; it does not retarget the defended graph. Run data (CSV) · Parameters and versions · Complete experiment script . Select a plot to open it at full size. A defended curve can retain more nodes both by avoiding fragmentation and by blocking removals. Its horizontal axis therefore counts attempts, not successful deletions. That distinction is essential when comparing this example with an attacker allowed to retarget protected nodes. After eight attempts, mean retained connectivity is 14.7% without protection, 27.9% with random protection, and 85.3% with NetShield. These karate-club runs use the supplied weights for NetShield selection; the attack uses unweighted structure. Improve topology before damage This experiment changes the karate graph before damage occurs. Each policy receives five additions or rewiring actions, then faces the same eight-node recalculated-betweenness order computed on the intact graph. Unit weights keep the comparison focused on topology. The code copies the original graph for every trial, applies additions or sequential removal/addition pairs, and only then deletes the attacked nodes. Sequential rewiring preserves the intended edge budget even when a later edit touches an edge created earlier. The attacker does not observe the intervention or recompute its order. Run the preventive topology experiment — code Twenty seeds per policy; lines are means and bands show the 10th–90th percentiles. No defense is the baseline. Data (CSV) · Parameters · Rerun this study . Compare each curve with the no-defense baseline at the same number of removals. A gain means that these pre-positioned connections help against this specified attack order; it does not establish protection against an adaptive attacker. The before-and-after network views below show one of these five-edge interventions directly. The complete runner plots the stored results . A realistic design study should also filter infeasible links and evaluate several plausible attack realizations. See the defense itself The preventive topology experiment uses the 34-node karate-club social graph. We use unit edge weights and add five connections between low-degree endpoints. The before-and-after views isolate the intervention itself: no attack has occurred yet. The code computes one spring layout on the original graph, saves the returned additions, and draws the modified graph at the same positions. Orange dashed lines are new connections; gray lines already existed. The spring layout arranges nodes for readability, so line length is not a construction distance. The preventive experiment above tests whether these changes retain connectivity under its fixed attack. Draw the added connections — code The intervention is the one used in the preventive-defense guide. Data (CSV) · Parameters · Rerun this study . The picture shows which nodes receive alternatives, not whether those alternatives are effective under every threat. An added connection helps an attack outcome only if its endpoints survive and it connects useful parts of the remaining network. Shared plotting and export options . "},{"title":"Epidemic simulations","url":"epidemics.html","text":" Guide Simulate epidemics with SIS and SIR On this page Choose an epidemic model SIS: reinfection is possible SIR: recovery is lasting SI: the no-recovery limit Simulation rule and time scale Synchronous transitions Calibrate the time step Effective strength Initial conditions, runs, and outputs Interventions SIS studies Vaccination and transmission sweep Added-contact comparison SIR studies One SIR trajectory Repeated SIR outbreaks SIR network states Use SIS when recovery returns a node to susceptibility and reinfection is possible. Use SIR when recovery produces lasting immunity. These histories require different outcomes and interpretations. The classical SIR framework was introduced by Kermack and McKendrick (1927) . TIGER implements discrete-time network transitions; its per-step probabilities are distinct from the rates in the original continuous-time equations. Choose an epidemic model SIS: reinfection is possible Susceptible nodes can become infected, and recovered nodes immediately become susceptible again. Use SIS when infection can recur and current prevalence or extinction is the primary outcome. SIR: recovery is lasting Recovered nodes enter an absorbing state and cannot be infected again. Use SIR when the questions concern current prevalence, the timing of the outbreak, or the total fraction eventually infected. SI: the no-recovery limit SI permits transmission but no recovery, so the infected set can only grow. TIGER exposes SIS and SIR as named models; use model=\"SIS\" with d=0 for the same SI state transitions. model=\"SI\" is not an accepted value. Setting Model Report Interpretation No lasting immunity SIS Infected fraction over time; persistence/extinction A finite realization reaching zero cannot restart without external infection. Lasting immunity SIR Prevalence and final recovered fraction Final recovered fraction is outbreak size, not current prevalence. Parameters. b is the per-step transmission probability to each susceptible neighbor, d is the per-step recovery probability for each infected node, and c is the initially infected fraction. State what one time step represents. sis_curve contains mean infected counts; sir_curve contains mean recovered counts. Divide by len(G) to obtain fractions. A SIR prevalence curve requires infected counts from the per-step simulation state. SIS: infection may persist time infected SIR: outbreak ends time recovered infected Schematic trajectories, not simulation results. SIS permits reinfection; SIR recovery accumulates as the outbreak ends. Simulation rule and time scale Synchronous transitions TIGER uses synchronous discrete-time updates. If a susceptible node has m infected neighbors at the beginning of a step, independent transmission attempts give infection probability 1 − (1−b) m . Nodes already infected recover independently with probability d. All changes are then applied together, so newly infected nodes begin transmitting on the next step. Recovery returns a node to S in SIS or to the absorbing R state in SIR. Calibrate the time step The probabilities b and d apply once per simulation step, so define what one step represents. Reusing a daily probability in an hourly simulation creates 24 opportunities per day and changes the process. Under constant transmission and recovery rates λ and μ, convert rates to probabilities for a step of duration Δt: b Δ t = 1 − e − λ Δ t d Δ t = 1 − e − μ Δ t TIGER does not perform this conversion or attach physical units to a step; supply probabilities calibrated to the time scale of the study. Effective strength s = λ max ( A ) b d , d > 0 The value combines network connectivity with transmission relative to recovery. A threshold near s=1 comes from a linearized mean-field description; it is not an exact persistence guarantee for a finite stochastic network. A finite closed SIS process can eventually go extinct even when it remains active for a long time. get_effective_strength() computes the score from the simulation graph. With node vaccination, vaccinated nodes remain in that graph, so the score does not automatically measure the susceptible contact subgraph. Initial conditions, runs, and outputs c is the initially infected fraction; TIGER selects floor(c × n) nodes uniformly at random before applying vaccination. run_single_sim() returns one trajectory and leaves its per-step state in sim_info . run_simulation() runs the requested number of realizations and returns their mean primary trajectory. For SIS, the primary trajectory is the current infected count. For SIR, it is the recovered count; read sim_info[t][\"failed\"] for current infection. Use repeated runs when reporting extinction probability, peak prevalence, outbreak size, or uncertainty. A mean curve alone does not show the distribution of outcomes. Interventions To suppress infection, use node selection for vaccination or edge removal to reduce contacts. To promote a desirable diffusion process, use edge addition or rewiring to create transmission opportunities. Compare interventions with the same model, graph, time scale, initial infection rule, run count, and action budget. SIS studies Vaccination and transmission sweep Compare no vaccination, random vaccination, and NetShield vaccination at the same budget. Vary b while keeping recovery, graph, initial infection fraction, duration, and run count fixed. The contact network is one fixed 100-node Barabási–Albert graph (m=3, graph seed 17). We compare no vaccination, five randomly vaccinated nodes, and five NetShield-selected nodes. Transmission probabilities are 0.02, 0.04, and 0.08 per infected–susceptible contact per step; recovery probability is 0.2. For each setting, the code runs 20 seeds for 150 steps with an initial infection fraction of 0.1. It reads infected counts from the SIS trajectory and divides by 100. Compare the same transmission color across the three panels. Vaccinated initial infections are removed rather than replaced, so the intervention can change both initial prevalence and later spread. Run the SIS vaccination study — code Means and 10th–90th percentile ranges across seeds 0–19; b=0.02, 0.04, 0.08, d=0.2, c=0.1. All panels share the same axes. Vaccinated initial infections are removed rather than replaced, so vaccination can change both initial prevalence and subsequent transmission. Run data (CSV) · Parameters and versions · Complete experiment script . Select a plot to open it at full size. Higher curves mean more infection is present at that time, not that more distinct people have ever been infected: SIS permits reinfection. Shading shows variability between runs, and a finite-time plateau is not proof of indefinite persistence. At b=0.04, mean prevalence at step 150 is 17.4% without vaccination, 15.3% with random vaccination, and 0.0% with NetShield. These finite-horizon results are not universal epidemic thresholds. Optional larger AS-733 benchmark This longer setup is retained for further study; the figures above are not its results. The optional benchmark uses 5,000 steps on AS-733. The as_733 loader selects the AS-733 snapshot, which is distinct from the separately available Oregon-1 dataset. Initially selected infected nodes that are vaccinated are removed from the infected set, so realized initial infection counts can differ between interventions. Added-contact comparison Keep the karate network and SIS probabilities fixed (transmission 0.08, recovery 0.2, initial infection fraction 0.1), then compare its original contacts with five randomly added edges. The question is whether more contact opportunities increase current prevalence, which may be desirable for information spread but harmful for infection. The code adds contacts before each simulation and runs both conditions for 150 steps over 20 seeds. It averages infected fractions at each step. Only edge addition is used here; this is not a comparison of all possible rewiring or targeting policies. Run the added-contact comparison — code Twenty runs, seeds 0–19, b=0.08, d=0.2 and c=0.1. The same parameters are used with and without five added edges. Shading shows run-to-run variability; this small experiment does not establish a universally beneficial intervention. Run data (CSV) · Parameters and versions · Complete experiment script . Select a plot to open it at full size. The mean curves summarize the observed direction, while their overlapping run ranges show that individual outcomes vary substantially. A change in mean prevalence is not a universal guarantee for every seed or every network. Mean step-150 prevalence is 32.2% without added links and 38.4% with five random added links. Wide run ranges caution against judging an intervention from a single realization. SIR studies One SIR trajectory This small example uses the 34-node karate contact graph, three initially infected nodes, transmission probability 0.08, and recovery probability 0.2. There is no vaccination. Simulation seed 17 selects one realization, followed for 150 steps. For SIR, the returned trajectory counts recovered nodes rather than currently infected nodes. The code therefore reads both fields from the step history, divides by 34, and obtains the susceptible fraction as one minus infected minus recovered. The three curves describe mutually exclusive states and sum to one. Run and read one SIR realization — code A single realization, seed 17, on the 34-node karate-club graph. b=0.08, d=0.2, c=0.1; no vaccination. Susceptible, infected and recovered fractions sum to one at every step. The recovered curve accumulates past infections; it is not current prevalence. Run data (CSV) · Parameters and versions · Complete experiment script . Select a plot to open it at full size. In this realization the initially infected nodes recover without transmitting onward. That is a valid stochastic fadeout, not evidence that these parameters always prevent an outbreak. The repeated-run section below tests how representative this single outcome is. This realization finishes with 8.8% recovered and 0.0% infected. The repeated-run study below places the single trajectory in its outbreak-size distribution. With pre-vaccination, the recovered field includes vaccinated nodes. Subtract the initially vaccinated count when measuring cumulative recoveries caused by the simulated outbreak. If infection remains at the last step, the final recovered fraction is not yet the completed outbreak size. Repeated SIR outbreaks Use the same 34-node karate topology and SIR probabilities as the single-run example, explicitly set all contact weights to one, and run seeds 0–99. Simulations continue for 300 steps so the recorded recovered fraction can be interpreted as completed outbreak size only after infection has disappeared. The code collects two different outputs: one final recovered fraction per run for the histogram, and a full infected-fraction trajectory per run for the prevalence plot. Histogram height counts runs; the time-series line is mean current infection and its band is the 10th–90th percentile range. Only the first 80 time steps are displayed, but the CSV includes all 301 recorded states. Run the repeated SIR experiment — code Seeds 0–99; b=0.08, d=0.2, c=0.1. The prevalence plot shows the first 80 of 300 simulated steps. Full prevalence trajectories (CSV) contain all 301 timesteps for each of the 100 seeds, sufficient to reconstruct the mean and percentile band. The data link below contains final outbreak sizes. Data (CSV) · Parameters · Rerun this study . These panels answer complementary questions: how large an outbreak ultimately becomes, and when infection is present. A modest mean prevalence curve can coexist with large final outbreaks because infections occur at different times and recovered nodes accumulate. 5 of 100 runs produced no secondary infections. Mean final outbreak size was 53.1%. All runs had zero infected nodes at the last step. Reporting the distribution avoids confusing one fadeout with the typical outcome. For the spectral effective-strength heuristic, use a spectral radius consistent with the contact model’s weights. Reference SIR network states This illustration uses a synthetic 60-node Barabási–Albert contact graph, with two links introduced per new node and graph seed 17. Initially six nodes are infected. Each infected–susceptible contact transmits with probability 0.15 per step, and infected nodes recover with probability 0.08. We inspect steps 0, 3, 10, and 30 of simulation seed 17. The code stores one layout and obtains each state by rerunning that same seeded realization to the requested step. It colors each node from its state at that time: teal is susceptible, orange infected, and blue recovered. The plotting loop below uses the stored colors without recomputing positions. These are topological contact locations, not geographic locations. Generate and draw the SIR states — code Graph seed 17, simulation seed 17; b=0.15, d=0.08, c=0.1. This is a separate illustration, not the karate outbreak-size study. Data (CSV) · Parameters · Rerun this study . At the four displayed steps, infected counts are 6, 21, 29, 3 and recovered counts are 0, 3, 21, 51. Orange nodes show current infection, while blue nodes record past infections that have recovered. A decline in orange therefore need not mean a small outbreak: many nodes may already be blue. This illustration uses a different graph and parameters from the karate-club outbreak-size experiment. Shared plotting and export options . "},{"title":"Original figures","url":"historical-figures.html","text":" Earlier tutorial figures These archived figures accompanied earlier tutorials and are retained for reference. Use the current guide figures and downloadable experiment settings for results produced by the documented workflows. Measure categories Robustness measure overview Approximation errors Failure and attack overview KY-2 attack snapshots KY-2 node and edge attack comparison Defense categories KY-2 defense comparison SIS network snapshots SIS vaccination and strength comparison The original introduction also contains cascade and vaccination time-series snapshots. View the original introduction and its figure links. "},{"title":"Introduction","url":"index.html","text":" TIGER documentation Study how network structure, disruptions, and interventions shape system behavior. On this page What you can study From a graph to a defensible result Where to go next TIGER is a Python toolkit for robustness, vulnerability, and propagation experiments on NetworkX graphs. It connects structural measures with node and edge attacks, defenses, epidemics, influence processes, and cascading failures so that each result answers a stated operational question. Start with the study question. Robustness is not one universal score. Specify what can fail or spread, what outcome represents service, and what attack or intervention budget is available. Then select the model and measures that match those choices. What you can study Measure network structure Evaluate connectivity, reachability, redundancy, path concentration, and spectral structure—and understand what each measure can and cannot establish. Stress-test the network Compare random and targeted node or edge attacks under the same removal budget, including policies that recalculate priorities as damage accumulates. Evaluate interventions Test node protection, edge addition, and rewiring against a fixed threat model rather than assuming that a structural improvement produces an operational benefit. Simulate epidemics Run synchronous SIS and SIR processes on contact networks and compare intervention outcomes over repeated realizations. Simulate information diffusion Compare independent-cascade, threshold, voter, and competitive-message dynamics under explicit update rules. Simulate cascading failures Use global rerouting or local load sharing when component failures redistribute load through the network. From a graph to a defensible result Load and check the graph. Use a built-in network, a generator, or your own NetworkX graph. Confirm direction, weights, connectedness, and node attributes before analysis. Define the outcome. Choose a quantity tied to retained service, such as largest-component size, reachability, efficiency, epidemic prevalence, or failed load. Specify the disruption. State the failure, attack, epidemic, or cascade mechanism and its budget or parameters. Choose a comparison. Compare with a random baseline, an alternative attack, or a defense evaluated on the same graph and under the same conditions. Repeat and report. Use multiple runs for stochastic methods, preserve seeds and settings, and show trajectories or distributions rather than only one endpoint. Where to go next Begin with installation and first steps , then learn how to load graphs and choose robustness measures . Continue to the attack, defense, epidemic, influence, or cascade guide that matches the process you need to study. The visualization guide shows how to inspect network states and results, while the reproducibility guide records the conventions used by the documentation experiments. "},{"title":"Information diffusion","url":"influence.html","text":" Information diffusion and influence On this page Independent cascade Linear threshold Voter model Competitive cascades Runs, history, and stopping Use these models when a node represents an adopter, opinion, message, or decision rather than an infected individual. The model determines whether activation is permanent, whether several neighbors reinforce one another, whether states can reverse, and how competing messages resolve a simultaneous arrival. Model Node change Update schedule Primary result Independent cascade An active node gets one probabilistic attempt to activate each inactive target. Synchronous rounds Total active nodes Linear threshold A node activates when accumulated incoming influence reaches its threshold. Synchronous rounds Total active nodes Voter One selected node copies one selected neighbor, so either state can replace the other. One asynchronous node update per step Nodes in the tracked state Competitive cascade The first message to activate a node is permanent; a declared rule resolves same-round collisions. Synchronous rounds Nodes holding the tracked message The four models share the Influence class, but their parameters are not interchangeable. Directed independent, threshold, and competitive cascades move along outgoing edges. The voter implementation currently requires an undirected graph. Independent cascade Choose this model when each newly active source has one opportunity to transmit through each eligible edge. All attempts in a round read the same starting state. Successful targets form the next frontier and may attempt transmission in the following round; a failed edge is not retried by that source. The model convention follows Kempe, Kleinberg, and Tardos . probability may be one number for every edge or the name of an edge attribute. With probability 1 in the figure, B activates in round 1 and only then attempts C and D in round 2. This deliberately deterministic setting exposes the update order; realistic probabilities require repeated runs. Orange nodes are active. Newly active nodes do not transmit until the next round. Data (CSV) · Parameters · Rerun this study . Linear threshold Choose this model when several active neighbors can jointly produce adoption. Each active predecessor contributes its edge weight once. An inactive node becomes active when the accumulated incoming weight is at least its threshold, and activation is permanent. This is the weighted threshold convention described by Kempe, Kleinberg, and Tardos . The two incoming weights at C sum to 0.70, exceeding its 0.60 threshold; neither 0.35 contribution would activate C alone. C therefore activates in round 1 and its weight of 1.0 activates D in round 2. threshold may be a scalar, a node-to-value mapping, or a node-attribute name. weight names the edge attribute and missing values default to 1. Edge labels show influence weights; the annotation below C shows its activation threshold. Data (CSV) · Parameters · Rerun this study . Voter model Choose the voter model when opinions or labels can reverse. At each step, TIGER samples one node and then one of its neighbors uniformly; the selected node copies that neighbor’s current state immediately. One step is one node-update event, not a synchronous sweep over the graph. The process follows the local copying model of Clifford and Sudbury . The example begins with equal-sized contiguous regions. Individual runs can move toward either consensus state, so the ensemble mean alone is incomplete. The network panels show one realization; the curve reports the mean and 10th–90th percentile interval over 40 independent seeds. Isolated nodes retain their state. Orange and gray mark the two states. The wide band reflects stochastic local copying, not measurement error. Data (CSV) · Parameters · Rerun this study . Competitive cascades Use a competitive cascade when several messages spread at once and adoption is permanent. Each message has its own frontier. A previously inactive node first collects every successful same-round proposal; it then adopts one message using either a random tie break or the supplied priority order. Resolving proposals after the round prevents node-iteration order from deciding the winner. The implementation uses the competitive diffusion setting introduced by Bharathi, Kempe, and Salek with this explicit collision rule. Both messages advance one edge per round. At round 4 they reach node 4 simultaneously, so the declared priority assigns that node to the correction. A random tie rule samples among the messages that actually reached the node. probability may also map each message to its own scalar or edge-attribute name. Red is the claim, blue is the correction, and gray is inactive. The priority rule—not drawing order—settles the center node. Data (CSV) · Parameters · Rerun this study . Runs, history, and stopping run_single_sim() returns steps + 1 values and leaves that realization in sim_info . run_simulation() averages the same primary trajectory over runs . Progressive models return active-node counts; voter and competitive models return the count for tracked_state . Divide by the graph order when a fraction is required. For each step, sim_info[t][\"status\"] follows the graph’s node iteration order, counts reports all states, and changed identifies nodes changed in that update. Progressive and competitive cascades stop changing when no frontier remains; the fixed-length return carries the terminal value forward. The voter model stops changing only at an absorbing state, such as consensus within every connected component. A finite step limit can end first, so report both the limit and the final state. These simulations evaluate diffusion from user-supplied starting nodes. They do not solve the separate optimization problem of choosing a seed set to maximize expected reach. "},{"title":"Installation","url":"installation.html","text":" Installation and first steps On this page Load a graph and measure it Environment and optional dependencies Loaders and troubleshooting Install TIGER in a Python environment, then start with a small graph before running a larger empirical study. Optional ForceAtlas2 layouts and edge bundling use the visualization extra. Load a graph and measure it Supported empirical loaders include ky2 , electrical , and as_733 . Dataset availability and preprocessing vary by loader; consult the graphs API . TIGER returns undirected NetworkX graphs. Environment and optional dependencies The package supports Python 3.8 and later. These documented results were run on Python 3.12 with versions recorded in the environment lock . Use an isolated environment and start with a small graph. Basic plotting does not need ForceAtlas2 or edge bundling. The visualization extra can require a compiler for optional dependencies. If it fails, use NetworkX spring or spectral layouts. Native MP4 export is unavailable on Windows; static figures remain available. Laplacian GPU calculations are unavailable, while optional adjacency-spectrum calculations require a working CuPy/CUDA environment and a partial-spectrum request. Loaders and troubleshooting See Loading graphs for cache paths, graph conversions, and weights. A disconnected-graph measure error is different from a download failure; check the selected measure’s input conventions before changing the model. "},{"title":"Robustness measures","url":"measures.html","text":" Guide Robustness measures On this page Measure conventions at a glance Three worked measure definitions Average vertex betweenness Spectral scaling Effective graph resistance Approximation and runtime Approximation error and computation time The outcome should determine the measure—not the other way around. A small complementary set is often more defensible than one universal score. Question Primary measure Caution How much remains mutually reachable? largest_connected_component Does not describe distances inside the component. Can destinations still be reached efficiently? average_inverse_distance Assumes shortest paths represent service. How many removals disconnect the graph? node_connectivity / edge_connectivity A minimum cut is not typical damage. Are there alternative routes? natural_connectivity , number_spanning_trees Structural redundancy may not equal operational capacity. Is routing concentrated? vertex/edge betweenness An average can conceal extreme components. The reference also documents diameter, average distance, clustering, spectral radius and gap, spectral scaling, generalized robustness index, algebraic connectivity, and effective resistance. Measure conventions at a glance These conventions describe TIGER 0.7.0; they are not universal guarantees about network robustness. “Exact” distinguishes full calculations from sampling or truncation, not exact arithmetic. Reference Measure key Output / normalization Disconnected graphs Weights / approximation largest_connected_component Nodes; divide by original n for attack curves Valid; largest remaining component No weights; exact node_connectivity / edge_connectivity Minimum removal count 0 when already disconnected No weights; exact diameter Maximum hop distance Undefined; dispatcher can return None No weights; exact average_distance Mean hop distance Undefined; dispatcher can return None No weights; exact; rounded to 2 decimals average_inverse_distance Mean reciprocal hop distance (global efficiency) Unreachable pairs contribute 0 No weights; exact; 2 decimals average_vertex_betweenness Mean unnormalized brokerage over nodes Unreachable pairs contribute 0 No weights; k sampled source nodes; 2 decimals average_edge_betweenness Mean unnormalized brokerage over edges Unreachable pairs contribute 0; edgeless result 0 No weights; k sampled source nodes; 2 decimals average_clustering_coefficient Mean local clustering, in [0,1] Valid; isolated nodes contribute 0 No weights; exact; 2 decimals spectral_radius Largest adjacency eigenvalue Defined, but not a connectivity indicator alone Uses weight attribute; 2 decimals spectral_gap Largest minus second-largest adjacency eigenvalue Can remain positive despite disconnection Uses weight attribute; 2 decimals natural_connectivity Log mean exponential adjacency eigenvalue Defined; does not require connectedness Uses weight attribute; k retained eigenvalues; 2 decimals spectral_scaling Log-scale residual; a structural diagnostic May be undefined, especially with zero odd-walk centrality Uses weight attribute; k retained eigenpairs generalized_robustness_index Truncated spectral-scaling residual Same limitations as spectral scaling Uses weight attribute; default k=30 for direct function algebraic_connectivity Second-smallest Laplacian eigenvalue 0 for disconnected nonnegative-weight graphs Uses weights as conductances; 2 decimals number_spanning_trees Tree count, or weighted tree-product sum 0 when disconnected Full spectrum for matrix-tree identity; partial product is not reliable effective_resistance Sum over unordered-pair resistances Infinity when disconnected; singleton 0 Weights are conductances, not lengths; partial spectrum underestimates; 2 decimals The dispatcher supplies its default k=∞ even to functions with a different direct-call default. Specify k explicitly when that distinction matters. Three worked measure definitions Average vertex betweenness For an undirected graph with n = |V| nodes, let σ st count shortest paths between distinct endpoints s and t, and σ st (u) count those passing through u. Exclude u as an endpoint and count each unordered pair once. b ( u ) = ∑ s < t s , t ≠ u σ s t ( u ) σ s t b ¯ = 1 n ∑ u ∈ V b ( u ) Disconnected pairs contribute zero. TIGER averages unnormalized betweenness over nodes. A smaller average can reflect shorter routes or lost reachability; it does not establish that routing load is more evenly distributed. Spectral scaling Spectral scaling compares each node’s leading-eigenvector value with its participation in odd closed walks. For adjacency eigenpairs (λ j , u j ), define odd subgraph centrality, a scaling constant C, and each node’s logarithmic residual eᵢ. Spectral scaling ξ is the root-mean-square residual: SC odd ( i ) = ∑ j = 1 n u j ( i ) 2 sinh ( λ j ) C = [ sinh ( λ 1 ) ] − 1 2 e i = log 10 ( | u 1 ( i ) | C SC odd ( i ) ) ξ = 1 n ∑ i = 1 n e i 2 This is a structural diagnostic of the proposed scaling relationship. Bipartite graphs have no odd closed walks, making the logarithm undefined. TIGER can return None for nonfinite results; do not treat this as zero vulnerability. Its implementation orders eigenpairs by absolute eigenvalue magnitude, which also merits care on bipartite graphs. Effective graph resistance Treat every edge as a unit resistor. If the connected graph’s Laplacian eigenvalues are 0 = μ 1 < μ 2 ≤ … ≤ μ n , the sum of effective resistances over unordered node pairs is: R = ∑ i < j R i j = n ∑ j = 2 n 1 μ j Parallel routes reduce resistance. Adding a positive-conductance edge to a connected graph decreases total resistance. TIGER returns infinity for a disconnected graph. Compare like-sized networks, or state a size normalization. Approximation and runtime The parameter k has a different meaning for different measures: sampled nodes for betweenness and retained eigenvalues for spectral measures. Use the original measure name with k ; names ending in _approx are not accepted method keys. Measure Role of k How to interpret the result Node and edge betweenness Number of sampled source nodes Each run estimates betweenness from a sample, so results can vary between samples. Natural connectivity Number of retained adjacency eigenvalues The truncated exponential sum omits contributions from the remaining eigenvalues. Effective resistance Number of retained nonzero Laplacian eigenvalues Omitting positive reciprocal terms underestimates the full sum. Spanning-tree count Number of retained Laplacian eigenvalues A partial spectral product is not the matrix-tree identity and is not generally a reliable estimate. For graphs smaller than 100 nodes, the current eigensolver uses a full dense spectrum even when a smaller k is supplied. Benchmark larger graphs to observe spectral truncation. The example below evaluates five representative measures and compares each approximation with its exact calculation. We generate five clustered scale-free graphs with 300 nodes, m=3, triangle probability 0.3, and seeds 0–4. On each graph, a full calculation provides a separate reference for each measure. The comparison varies k over 10, 30, 60, 100, 200, and 300 without changing that graph. The code times each estimate and computes its absolute difference from the full value on the same graph before averaging across graphs. For betweenness, k controls sampled sources; for spectral measures, it controls retained eigenvalues. Each figure pair shows error first and runtime second (side by side on wide screens). Their vertical scales differ between measures, so compare k values within a measure. Run the error and timing comparison — code Approximation error and computation time Measured with TIGER 0.7.0 on five clustered scale-free graphs (300 nodes, m = 3, triangle probability 0.3; seeds 0–4). Each point averages five measurements. Errors compare each graph with its full calculation before averaging. Runtime is machine-dependent; k = 300 requests a full calculation. Download figure (PDF) · Measurements (CSV) · Experiment settings Seek a useful accuracy–runtime tradeoff rather than the smallest k in isolation. The spanning-tree partial product is not a valid general substitute for the full matrix-tree calculation, and a zero error at returned precision can result from rounding. Runtime describes this measured environment, not a platform-independent speed guarantee. Reading the results. Betweenness error generally falls as more source nodes are sampled, while runtime rises. Spectral runtime is not monotonic: at this graph size the full dense calculation can outperform partial eigensolvers. Spanning-tree error stays extremely large until the full spectrum is used. Natural-connectivity error reaches zero at TIGER’s returned precision; this does not establish equality before rounding. Compute absolute error separately on each graph, then average the errors. Taking an absolute difference after averaging estimates can hide cancellation. TIGER rounds several returned measures, so inspect rounding before interpreting very small errors. "},{"title":"References","url":"references.html","text":" References Cite the software version and the model or algorithm used in the study. These sources describe the underlying methods; the guides specify TIGER’s implementation conventions. Baras, John S and Hovareshti, Pedram (2009). Efficient and robust communication topologies for distributed decision making in networked systems. Proceedings of the 48h IEEE Conference on Decision and Control (CDC) held jointly with 2009 28th Chinese Control Conference . Beygelzimer, Alina and Grinstein, Geoffrey and Linsker, Ralph and Rish, Irina (2005). Improving network robustness by edge modification. Physica A: Statistical Mechanics and its Applications . Bharathi, Shishir and Kempe, David and Salek, Mahyar (2007). Competitive Influence Maximization in Social Networks . Internet and Network Economics, WINE 2007, 306–311 . Brandes, Ulrik (2001). A faster algorithm for betweenness centrality. Journal of mathematical sociology . Chan, Hau and Akoglu, Leman and Tong, Hanghang (2014). Make it or break it: Manipulating robustness in large networks. Proceedings of the 2014 SIAM International Conference on Data Mining . Chan, Hau and Akoglu, Leman (2016). Optimizing network robustness by edge rewiring: a general framework. Data Mining and Knowledge Discovery . Chen, Chen and Tong, Hanghang and Prakash, B Aditya and Tsourakakis, Charalampos E and Eliassi-Rad, Tina and Faloutsos, Christos and Chau, Duen Horng (2015). Node immunization on large graphs: Theory and algorithms. IEEE Transactions on Knowledge and Data Engineering . Clifford, Peter and Sudbury, Aidan (1973). A Model for Spatial Conflict . Biometrika, 60(3), 581–588 . Crucitti, Paolo and Latora, Vito and Marchiori, Massimo (2004). Model for cascading failures in complex networks . Physical Review E . Ellens, Wendy and Spieksma, FM and Van Mieghem, P and Jamakovic, A and Kooij, RE (2011). Effective graph resistance. Linear algebra and its applications . Ellens, Wendy and Kooij, Robert E (2013). Graph measures and network robustness . arXiv preprint arXiv:1311.5064 . Esfahanian, Abdol--Hossein (2013). Connectivity algorithms. Topics in structural graph theory . Estrada, Ernesto and Rodr\\'\\iguez-Vel\\'azquez, Juan A (2005). Spectral measures of bipartivity in complex networks. Physical Review E . Estrada, Ernesto (2006). Network robustness to targeted attacks. The interplay of expansibility and degree distribution. The European Physical Journal B-Condensed Matter and Complex Systems . Fiedler, Miroslav (1973). Algebraic connectivity of graphs. Czechoslovak mathematical journal . Freitas, Scott and Yang, Diyi and Kumar, Srijan and Tong, Hanghang and Chau, Duen Horng (2021). Evaluating Graph Vulnerability and Robustness using TIGER . ACM CIKM . Ghosh, Arpita and Boyd, Stephen and Saberi, Amin (2008). Minimizing effective resistance of a graph. SIAM review . Holme, Petter and Kim, Beom Jun and Yoon, Chang No and Han, Seung Kee (2002). Attack vulnerability of complex networks . Physical review E . Kempe, David and Kleinberg, Jon and Tardos, Éva (2003). Maximizing the Spread of Influence through a Social Network . Proceedings of the Ninth ACM SIGKDD International Conference on Knowledge Discovery and Data Mining . Kermack, William Ogilvy and McKendrick, Anderson G (1927). A contribution to the mathematical theory of epidemics . Proceedings of the royal society of london. Series A, Containing papers of a mathematical and physical character . Malliaros, Fragkiskos D and Megalooikonomou, Vasileios and Faloutsos, Christos (2012). Fast robustness estimation in large social graphs: Communities and anomaly detection. Proceedings of the 2012 SIAM International Conference on Data Mining . Motter, Adilson E. and Lai, Ying-Cheng (2002). Cascade-based attacks on complex networks . Physical Review E, 66, 065102 . Page, Lawrence and Brin, Sergey and Motwani, Rajeev and Winograd, Terry (1999). The pagerank citation ranking: Bringing order to the web. . . Tong, Hanghang and Prakash, B Aditya and Tsourakakis, Charalampos and Eliassi-Rad, Tina and Faloutsos, Christos and Chau, Duen Horng (2010). On the vulnerability of large graphs . 2010 IEEE International Conference on Data Mining . Tong, Hanghang and Prakash, B Aditya and Eliassi-Rad, Tina and Faloutsos, Michalis and Faloutsos, Christos (2012). Gelling, and melting, large graphs by edge manipulation. Proceedings of the 21st ACM international conference on Information and knowledge management . Wang, Yang and Chakrabarti, Deepayan and Wang, Chenxi and Faloutsos, Christos (2003). Epidemic spreading in real networks: An eigenvalue viewpoint. 22nd International Symposium on Reliable Distributed Systems, 2003. Proceedings. . Wei, D. Q. and Luo, X. S. and Zhang, B. (2012). Analysis of cascading failure in complex power networks under the load local preferential redistribution rule . Physica A, 391, 2771–2777 . "},{"title":"Visualization","url":"visualization.html","text":" Visualization On this page Layouts and node states TIGER plotting options Result plots and export Compare the visualization options Node positions and layout iterations Straight versus bundled edges Selected transition snapshots Animation and individual frames Reproduce these comparisons Use network drawings to locate changes and result plots to measure their consequences. This guide covers layouts, node colors, edge styles, snapshots, and animation; complete examples and figures remain with the attack , defense , epidemic , and cascade studies. Layouts and node states Network-state figures now accompany their attack, defense, epidemic, or cascade experiment. Across all of them, compute positions once and reuse them across states; use physical coordinates only when supplied by the dataset. A spring layout shows connectivity, not geographic distance. Keep colors consistent and distinguish failed components from functioning links. Map colors to explicit states, such as susceptible, infected, and recovered, and use the same mapping at every step. Highlight added edges with a distinct line style; if failed nodes or original edges remain as context, make that clear in the legend. Avoid interpreting a layout’s apparent clusters or edge lengths as measured physical properties. TIGER plotting options Option Behavior plot_transition Saves selected network snapshots from the first simulation run. node_style= \"force_atlas\" Uses the optional ForceAtlas2 layout; fa_iter controls the number of layout iterations. edge_style= \"bundled\" Bundles nearby edges when the optional visualization dependencies are installed. gif_animation Writes an MP4 through FFmpeg on supported non-Windows platforms. gif_snaps Saves individual animation frames while the animation workflow runs. Result plots and export TIGER saves built-in plots under plots/ in the current working directory. The worked examples use Matplotlib for explicit axes, legends, and run-to-run bands. With Matplotlib, call fig.savefig(\"result.svg\", bbox_inches=\"tight\") before displaying the figure for scalable output, or use a .png filename with dpi=180 . A simulation trajectory includes steps + 1 states; label whether state zero is intact or already attacked, and state the normalization rather than relying on plotting defaults. Compare the visualization options Every drawing below uses the same unweighted karate graph: 34 nodes and 78 edges. The layout and edge-style comparisons change only the drawing, not the network or a robustness measure. Orange node 0 and purple node 33 are landmarks for following the same nodes between layouts; they are not failed or defended states. Colors acquire SIR meanings only in the state examples below. Node positions and layout iterations Without complete node-position attributes, node_style=None uses a spectral layout. node_style=\"force_atlas\" uses ForceAtlas2, and fa_iter sets its iteration count. Complete pos attributes take precedence over either setting: the circular example supplies positions deliberately and is not a map. Default spectral layout; no supplied positions · PDF Supplied circular positions; retained even with ForceAtlas2 requested · PDF ForceAtlas2 after 20 iterations · PDF ForceAtlas2 after 200 iterations · PDF Compare the separation of nodes and the ease of tracing connections—not the apparent geographic shape. Spectral positions can place some nodes very close together. More ForceAtlas2 iterations change the layout but do not add links or demonstrate greater robustness. The iteration comparison fixes the initialization with fa2 seed 17; TIGER does not pass its simulation seed to the layout engine. Colors, line widths, and titles use common documentation styling rather than TIGER’s default plot formatting. Try the node-layout options — code Each setting saves into its own directory under plots/ , retaining all three comparisons. Rerunning a setting replaces its own outputs. The downloadable gallery runner fixes ForceAtlas2 initialization explicitly and saves the coordinates used here. Straight versus bundled edges Both views below use the same 200-iteration ForceAtlas2 positions and identical axis limits. edge_style=None draws straight links; edge_style=\"bundled\" uses Datashader’s bundling routine to route nearby lines together. Straight links: easier to follow individual edges · PDF Bundled links: shared visual corridors, unchanged topology · PDF Bundling can reduce a dense tangle of lines, but can also make individual links harder to count. Curves joining visually are not new graph junctions: no nodes or edges were added. ForceAtlas2 and bundling require the optional visualization dependencies. Hold positions fixed and change edge style — code This compact example supplies spring positions; the figures use saved ForceAtlas2 positions. In either case, positions stay fixed and the two settings save into separate directories. Bundled edges currently require consecutive integer node labels aligned with graph iteration order. Use straight edges when the graph has arbitrary or reordered labels. Selected transition snapshots plot_transition=True saves selected states from the first run; it does not change the layout or dynamics. Here a SIR simulation uses b=0.2, d=0.08, c=0.15, seed 17, and 30 steps on the same karate graph. TIGER’s transition selector chooses steps 0, 1, 2, 25, 30. Its middle snapshot is chosen from the change in infected count, not necessarily halfway through elapsed time. Transition snapshot at step 0 · PDF Transition snapshot at step 1 · PDF Transition snapshot at step 2 · PDF Transition snapshot at step 25 · PDF Transition snapshot at step 30 · PDF Teal is susceptible, orange infected, and purple recovered. Positions stay fixed, while colors track the process. Fewer orange nodes late in the run can reflect recovery rather than a small total outbreak. The images use TIGER’s recorded states and transition selection with a consistent documentation palette. Save transition snapshots — code The example reruns the same seeded realization to each requested horizon, so every snapshot reads the state at its own time step while preserving a consistent layout. Animation and individual frames gif_animation=True requests TIGER’s MP4 export on supported platforms with FFmpeg installed. Despite the option name, that exporter creates an MP4, not a GIF. Adding gif_snaps=True also saves its individual animation frames as PDFs; it does not create another layout. For diffusion, the exporter uses every tenth step plus the final step, so these frame times differ from the transition snapshots above. Animation-frame state at step 0 · PDF Animation-frame state at step 10 · PDF Animation-frame state at step 20 · PDF Animation-frame state at step 30 · PDF Play a portable preview of these four states This Pillow-generated GIF uses the same recorded states, slowed to 800 ms per frame for inspection. It is a Windows-compatible preview, not output from TIGER’s native MP4 path. Close this panel to hide playback. Native animation export is unavailable on Windows, and gif_snaps does not export frames there. Static transition plots remain available. Use the gallery runner for the animated GIF shown here, or run native MP4 export on a supported non-Windows platform. Native animation and frame export — supported platforms only This example requests the native MP4 and individual frame outputs produced by TIGER. Reproduce these comparisons Install graph-tiger[visualization]==0.7.0 , then run run-visualization-gallery.py . Each SIR snapshot is rerun to its own horizon from the same seed, and the runner checks rendered color counts against the scalar trajectory. The gallery settings record dependency versions, graph and simulation settings, exact layout coordinates, selected frame times, and file hashes. The state-count CSV lets you compare snapshot colors with simulated counts. (function(){const routes={\"road-snapshots\":\"attacks.html#road-snapshots\",\"network-results\":\"attacks.html#network-results\",\"defense-snapshots\":\"defenses.html#defense-snapshots\",\"defense-network-results\":\"defenses.html#defense-network-results\",\"epidemic-snapshots\":\"epidemics.html#epidemic-snapshots\",\"epidemic-state-results\":\"epidemics.html#epidemic-state-results\",\"cascade-snapshots\":\"cascades.html#cascade-snapshots\",\"cascade-state-results\":\"cascades.html#cascade-state-results\",\"section-1\":\"visualization.html#plotting-options\"};function redirect(){const target=routes[decodeURIComponent(location.hash.slice(1))];if(target)location.replace(target);}addEventListener(\"hashchange\",redirect);redirect();})(); "},{"title":"Loading graphs","url":"network-inputs.html","text":" Loading graphs On this page What is supported out of the box? Generate a synthetic network Load a named dataset Dataset files and cache location Load a small toy graph Load your own network Read a two-column edge list Read CSV, weighted edges, GraphML, or GML Check representation before analysis Which operations read weights? TIGER works with NetworkX graph objects. You can generate a graph, load a named dataset or toy example through graph_loader , or read your own file with NetworkX and pass the resulting graph directly to TIGER. Your own graphs do not need to be registered with the loader. What is supported out of the box? TIGER 0.7.0 provides four generators, fourteen named datasets, and ten toy graphs. Names are case-sensitive. Print the installed version’s catalog rather than guessing a loader key: Generate a synthetic network Generator arguments are passed through to the selected model. Fix the graph seed to hold topology constant while comparing simulation settings. A simulation seed is separate: it controls events on that graph. Key Model Parameters Example arguments ER Erdős–Rényi independent-edge graph n: nodes; p: edge probability n=100, p=0.05, seed=17 WS Watts–Strogatz small-world graph n: nodes; m: ring neighbors per node; p: rewiring probability n=100, m=4, p=0.1, seed=17 BA Barabási–Albert preferential attachment n: nodes; m: edges per new node n=100, m=3, seed=17 CSF Clustered scale-free graph n: nodes; m: edges per new node; p: triangle-formation probability n=100, m=3, p=0.3, seed=17 Use valid NetworkX generator parameters: for example, BA and CSF require 1 ≤ m < n. In WS, m is the number of ring neighbors, not the number of edges added by a growing node; choose an even value for the intended symmetric ring degree. These generators need no dataset downloads. Load a named dataset The same loader reads each dataset below. Except for karate, it fetches the required file when it is absent from the selected dataset directory; subsequent loads reuse the file. Built-in does not mean every dataset is already bundled or that the first load works offline. Loader key Network Preprocessing / notes karate Zachary’s 34-node karate-club social network Available through NetworkX; no download. Preserves its supplied edge weights. ky2 Kentucky water-distribution network Junction-to-junction pipe links, node positions, integer relabeling, largest connected component. Not a road network or a hydraulic simulator. electrical Western-US power-grid topology Reads power.gml and retains its largest component; 4,941 nodes in the documented study. as_733 Internet autonomous systems, AS-733 snapshot as19971108 Largest component; integer relabeling. oregon_1 Internet autonomous systems, Oregon-1 Largest component; a different dataset from AS-733. p2p_gnuetella08 Gnutella peer-to-peer network Converts to undirected; largest component. Use this exact loader spelling. wiki_vote Wikipedia voting network Converts to undirected; largest component. Vote direction is discarded. email_eu_all EU email network Converts to undirected; largest component. enron_email Enron email network Undirected edge list; largest component. dblp DBLP coauthorship network Undirected edge list; largest component. ca_grqc General relativity / quantum cosmology collaborations Undirected edge list; largest component. ca_hep_th High-energy theory collaborations Undirected edge list; largest component. ca_astro_ph Astrophysics collaborations Undirected edge list; largest component. cit_hep_th High-energy theory citations Converts to undirected; largest component. Citation direction is discarded. Most empirical loaders retain only the largest connected component. That changes the population under study; their returned graphs are not necessarily the complete raw datasets. KY-2 contains 809 nodes in the documented experiments. Recorded dataset fingerprints and preprocessing identify the exact study inputs. Dataset files and cache location TIGER uses its bundled datasets directory if that directory exists; otherwise it uses ~/.graph_tiger/datasets under your user directory. The chosen directory must be writable for a missing-file download. Inspect the current location and source URLs, or select a writable directory before loading: get_graph_urls() maps downloadable keys to a file URL and its original source URL. For offline use, put the expected files in the selected directory first; for example, ky2.txt and power.gml . A missing file still requires network access. Dataset loaders do not use generator parameters such as n to select a smaller sample. Load a small toy graph These examples require no downloads and are useful for checking a measure or following individual simulation steps. Key Structure o4 Four isolated nodes p4 Four-node path s4 Four-node star c4 Four-node cycle k4-1 Four-node complete graph minus one edge k4-2 Four-node complete graph c4_no_bridge Two disconnected four-node cycles c4_1_bridge Two cycles joined by one link c4_2_bridge Two cycles joined by two links c4_3_bridge Two cycles joined by three parallel links; returns a MultiGraph The parallel-link example c4_3_bridge is an exception to the simple-graph convention. Its availability does not imply that every measure, attack, or simulation supports parallel edges. Use a simple undirected graph for the main workflows in these guides. Load your own network Read a two-column edge list For a whitespace-separated file with one pair of node labels per line, use nx.read_edgelist . This example treats links as unweighted and keeps every component represented in the file. An edge list cannot represent isolated nodes unless you load those node IDs separately and add them with G.add_nodes_from(node_ids) . The simulation example assumes a nonempty simple graph without self-loops. Inspect your input before selecting a process; do not silently drop components or change edge meaning to make a method run. Labels remain strings here, so the original identifiers are retained. Read CSV, weighted edges, GraphML, or GML Choose the reader that matches the file’s actual format. For a CSV with headers source,target , preserve identifier text explicitly: The following are alternative readers, not consecutive preprocessing steps. A weighted edge list contains three columns: source, target, and numeric weight. GraphML and GML can retain node and edge attributes, and may return directed or multigraph objects depending on the file. If you already have a NetworkX graph—from another generator, a database, or your own code—use that object directly. Preserve a node-order list such as node_order = list(G.nodes()) when interpreting saved state arrays. For spatial figures, store physical coordinates as node attributes, for example G.nodes[\"A\"][\"pos\"] = (x, y) , and state their units. Check representation before analysis Directed-to-undirected conversion discards direction; collapsing parallel edges discards multiplicity and needs an explicit rule for combining weights. Retaining only the largest component discards other nodes. Make these choices because they fit the study, not because the loader happens to do them. Disconnected graphs are valid inputs for some measures, but not for all path-based quantities. Which operations read weights? Operation TIGER 0.7.0 behavior Spectral measures and NetShield Read the NetworkX weight attribute; missing weights mean 1. Path measures, ID/IB/RD/RB attacks Use unweighted degree or hop-based paths. Edge-degree attack score is d(u)d(v). SIS and SIR Use one transmission probability b per infected–susceptible edge; ignore edge weights. Influence models Independent and competitive cascades may follow directed edges and edge-specific probabilities. Linear threshold reads edge weights and node thresholds. Voter currently requires an undirected graph. Motter–Lai Unweighted shortest-path betweenness; input edge weights are not traffic distances. Local load sharing Defaults use intact unweighted degree for initial load and recipient weights; supplied loads, capacities, failures, and headroom-based allocation policies are also supported. Crucitti Initial incident-edge efficiency is 1; simulated efficiencies determine routing distances. A spectral diagnostic computed from weighted contacts need not describe an unweighted epidemic on those same links. For an unweighted study, set every weight to 1 before both selection and simulation. Some karate-club examples retain NetworkX’s supplied weights; the preventive-defense and SIR-ensemble studies explicitly use unit weights. Graph loader API and source · Robustness measures "},{"title":"Reproduce the results","url":"reproducibility.html","text":" Reproduce and interpret results On this page Run the documented examples What is recorded? Repeated runs and uncertainty Outputs and history A seed identifies one random realization within a specified software environment. It is not a substitute for repeated runs, recorded graph preprocessing, or a declared stopping rule. Run the documented examples Download run-guide-studies.py , run-extended-studies.py , run-influence-guide.py , and run_guide_bootstrap.py into the same directory. Then create an environment and install the recorded dependency versions . The recorded environment used Python 3.12. On macOS/Linux use .venv/bin/python . Omitting defenses runs the five core guide studies; other choices are attacks , epidemics , cascades , and visualization . The influence runner reproduces its four model figures and shared result files. From a downloaded copy, it writes to site/guide-results relative to the runner; the copy already stored in guide-results writes beside itself. The approximation study has its own runner and renderer ; keep both together. What is recorded? Each figure links its observations and parameters. The manifest adds SciPy and other dependency versions, dataset SHA-256 hashes, preprocessing, and experiment-script hashes. Cross-version random streams and spectral calculations can differ. Rebuilt figures use saved observations where available; prose summaries are calculated from those observations during the documentation build. Repeated runs and uncertainty A Monte Carlo study samples independent realizations to estimate an outcome’s distribution. Our shaded bands are empirical 10th–90th percentiles, not confidence intervals for the mean. The same integer seed across interventions does not guarantee identical random exposure or transmission draws, because an intervention can consume random numbers or change the graph. Record realized initial infections when pairing comparisons. Outputs and history Call / process Returned value History run_simulation() Mean trajectory, steps+1 entries Resets after every realization, including the last. run_single_sim() One trajectory, steps+1 entries Read that realization before resetting; calling again continues current state rather than starting a fresh run. Attack / Defense / Motter–Lai / local load sharing Chosen measure; raw LCC is a node count Normalize by original n when tracking original-network service. SIS / SIR SIS: infected counts; SIR: recovered counts SIR recovered includes pre-vaccinated nodes. Influence Progressive models: active count. Voter and competition: tracked-state count. Per-step status follows graph node order; terminal states are carried through the fixed horizon. Crucitti Weighted efficiency over surviving pairs Report its denominator; it is not a functioning-node fraction. Mutable set fields such as SIR protected can share a live object with later states. Copy sets when capturing a transition, or rerun the same seed to a fixed horizon and read the current state. Counts and copied status lists are safer for time-series extraction. For layouts, plotting options, and export examples, see the Visualization guide . (function(){const anchors=[\"plotting-options\",\"visualization-layouts\",\"visualization-controls\",\"visualization-export\",\"visualization-compare\",\"visualization-layout-comparison\",\"visualization-edge-comparison\",\"visualization-transition-comparison\",\"visualization-animation-comparison\",\"visualization-gallery-files\"];function redirect(){const hash=decodeURIComponent(location.hash.slice(1));if(anchors.includes(hash))location.replace(\"visualization.html#\"+encodeURIComponent(hash));}addEventListener(\"hashchange\",redirect);redirect();})(); "},{"title":"Search documentation","url":"search.html","text":" Search documentation Search guides and API entries "},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.graph_loader(graph_type, **kwargs)","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-graph_loader","text":"Loads any of the available graph models, supported user-downloaded datasets and toy graphs.\nIn order to get a list of available graph options run 'get_graph_options()'.\n\n:param graph_type: a string representing the graph you want to load. For example, 'ER', 'WS', 'BA',\n       'oregon_1' (must first download), 'electrical' (must first download)\n:param kwargs: allows user to specify specific graph model properties\n:return: an undirected NetworkX graph"},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.download_dataset(dataset)","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-download_dataset","text":"Reading the dataset from the web.\n\n:param dataset: a string representing the dataset to download"},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.get_graph_urls()","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-get_graph_urls","text":"Returns a dictionary of the datasets used in TIGER and the original link to download them\n\n:return: dictionary containing links to each dataset"},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.get_graph_options()","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-get_graph_options","text":"Returns a formatted string containing all of the generators, datasets and custom graphs implemented in TIGER\n\n:return: formatted string"},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.erdos_reyni(n, p=None, seed=None)","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-erdos_reyni","text":"Returns a Erdos Reyni NetworkX graph\n\n:param n: number of nodes\n:param p: probability for edge creation\n:param seed: fixes the graph generation process\n:return: a NetworkX graph"},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.watts_strogatz(n, m=4, p=0.05, seed=None)","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-watts_strogatz","text":"Returns an ordinary Watts Strogatz NetworkX graph\n\n:param n: number of nodes\n:param m: each node is joined with its k nearest neighbors in a ring topology\n:param p: probability of rewiring each edge\n:param seed: fixes the graph generation process\n:return: a NetworkX graph"},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.barabasi_albert(n, m=3, seed=None)","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-barabasi_albert","text":"Returns a Barabasi Albert NetworkX graph\n\n:param n: number of nodes\n:param m: number of edges to attach from a new node to existing nodes\n:param seed: fixes the graph generation process\n:return: a NetworkX graph"},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.clustered_scale_free(n, m=3, p=0.3, seed=None)","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-clustered_scale_free","text":"Returns a Clustered Scale-Free NetworkX graph\n\n:param n: number of nodes\n:param m: the number of random edges to add for each new node\n:param p:  probability of adding a triangle after adding a random edge\n:param seed: fixes the graph generation process\n:return: a NetworkX graph"},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.wdn_ky2()","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-wdn_ky2","text":"Returns the graph from: https://uknowledge.uky.edu/wdst/4/,\nwhere we preprocess it to only keep the largest connected component\n\n:return: undirected NetworkX graph"},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.as_733()","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-as_733","text":"Returns the 'as19971108' graph from: http://snap.stanford.edu/data/as-733.html,\nwhere we preprocess it to only keep the largest connected component\n\n:return: undirected NetworkX graph"},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.p2p_gnuetella08()","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-p2p_gnuetella08","text":"Returns the graph from: https://snap.stanford.edu/data/p2p-Gnutella08.html,\nwhere we preprocess it to only keep the largest connected component\n\n:return: undirected NetworkX graph"},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.ca_grqc()","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-ca_grqc","text":"Returns the graph from: https://snap.stanford.edu/data/ca-GrQc.html,\nwhere we preprocess it to only keep the largest connected component\n\n:return: undirected NetworkX graph"},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.cit_hep_th()","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-cit_hep_th","text":"Returns the graph from: https://snap.stanford.edu/data/cit-HepTh.html,\nwhere we preprocess it to only keep the largest connected component\n\n:return: undirected NetworkX graph"},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.wiki_vote()","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-wiki_vote","text":"Returns the graph from: https://snap.stanford.edu/data/wiki-Vote.html,\nwhere we preprocess it to only keep the largest connected component\n\n:return: undirected NetworkX graph"},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.email_eu_all()","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-email_eu_all","text":"Returns the graph from: https://snap.stanford.edu/data/email-EuAll.html,\nwhere we preprocess it to only keep the largest connected component\n\n:return: undirected NetworkX graph"},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.dblp()","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-dblp","text":"Returns the graph from: https://snap.stanford.edu/data/com-DBLP.html,\nwhere we preprocess it to only keep the largest connected component\n\n:return: undirected NetworkX graph"},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.ca_astro_ph()","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-ca_astro_ph","text":"Returns the graph from: https://snap.stanford.edu/data/ca-AstroPh.html,\nwhere we preprocess it to only keep the largest connected component\n\n:return: undirected NetworkX graph"},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.ca_hep_th()","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-ca_hep_th","text":"Returns the graph from: https://snap.stanford.edu/data/cit-HepTh.html,\nwhere we preprocess it to only keep the largest connected component\n\n:return: undirected NetworkX graph"},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.enron_email()","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-enron_email","text":"Returns the graph from: https://snap.stanford.edu/data/email-Enron.html,\nwhere we preprocess it to only keep the largest connected component\n\n:return: undirected NetworkX graph"},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.karate()","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-karate","text":"Returns the graph from: https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/generated/networkx.generators.social.karate_club_graph.html,\n\n:return: undirected NetworkX graph"},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.oregeon_1()","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-oregeon_1","text":"Returns the graph from: https://snap.stanford.edu/data/oregon1_010331.html,\nwhere we preprocess it to only keep the largest connected component\n\n:return: undirected NetworkX graph"},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.electrical()","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-electrical","text":"Returns the graph from: http://konect.cc/networks/opsahl-powergrid/,\nwhere we preprocess it to only keep the largest connected component\n\n:return: undirected NetworkX graph"},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.o4_graph()","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-o4_graph","text":"Returns a 4 node disconnected graph\n\n:return: undirected NetworkX graph"},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.p4_graph()","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-p4_graph","text":"Returns a 4 node path graph\n\n:return: undirected NetworkX graph"},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.s4_graph()","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-s4_graph","text":"Returns a 4 node star graph\n\n:return: undirected NetworkX graph"},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.c4_graph()","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-c4_graph","text":"Returns a 4 node cycle graph\n\n:return: undirected NetworkX graph"},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.k4_1_graph()","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-k4_1_graph","text":"Returns a 4 node diamond graph (1 diagonal edge)\n\n:return: undirected NetworkX graph"},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.k4_2_graph()","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-k4_2_graph","text":"Returns a 4 node diamond graph (2 diagonal edges), a.k.a. complete graph\n\n:return: undirected NetworkX graph"},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.two_c4_0_bridge()","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-two_c4_0_bridge","text":"Returns two disconnected 4 node cycle graphs\n\n:return: undirected NetworkX graph"},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.two_c4_1_bridge()","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-two_c4_1_bridge","text":"Returns two 4 node cycle graphs connected by 1 edge\n\n:return: undirected NetworkX graph"},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.two_c4_2_bridge()","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-two_c4_2_bridge","text":"Returns two 4 node cycle graphs connected by 2 edges\n\n:return: undirected NetworkX graph"},{"title":"graph_tiger.graphs.two_c4_3_bridge()","url":"api-graphs.html#api-graphs-two_c4_3_bridge","text":"Returns two 4 node cycle graphs connected by 3 edges\n\n:return: undirected NetworkX graph"},{"title":"graph_tiger.measures.run_measure(graph, measure, k=np.inf, use_gpu=False, timeout=None)","url":"api-measures.html#api-measures-run_measure","text":"Evaluates graph robustness according to a specified measure\n\n:param graph: undirected NetworkX graph to measure\n:param measure: string containing the robustness measure to evaluate\n:param k: an integer for fast approximation of certain robustness measures. small k = fast, large k = precise\n:param timeout: optional number of seconds to wait for the measure.\n:return: a float representing the robustness of the graph, or None if it times out or a NetworkX error occurs"},{"title":"graph_tiger.measures.get_measures()","url":"api-measures.html#api-measures-get_measures","text":"Returns a list of strings representing all of the available graph robustness measures\n\n:return: list of strings"},{"title":"graph_tiger.measures.node_connectivity(graph, **kwargs)","url":"api-measures.html#api-measures-node_connectivity","text":"Measures the minimal number of vertices that can be removed to disconnect the graph.\nLarger vertex (node) connectivity --> harder to disconnect graph\n--> more robust graph .\n\n:param graph: undirected NetworkX graph\n:return: an integer"},{"title":"graph_tiger.measures.edge_connectivity(graph, **kwargs)","url":"api-measures.html#api-measures-edge_connectivity","text":"Measures the minimal number of edges that can be removed to disconnect the graph.\nLarger edge connectivity --> harder to disconnect graph -->\nmore robust graph .\n\n:param graph: undirected NetworkX graph\n:return: an integer"},{"title":"graph_tiger.measures.avg_distance(graph, **kwargs)","url":"api-measures.html#api-measures-avg_distance","text":"The average distance between all pairs of nodes in the graph.\nThe smaller the average shortest path distance, the more robust the graph.\nThis can be viewed through the lens of network connectivity i.e.,\nsmaller avg. distance --> better connected graph .\n\nUndefined for disconnected graphs.\n\n:param graph: undirected NetworkX graph\n:return: a float"},{"title":"graph_tiger.measures.avg_inverse_distance(graph, **kwargs)","url":"api-measures.html#api-measures-avg_inverse_distance","text":"The average inverse distance between all pairs of nodes in the graph.\nThe larger the average inverse shortest path distance, the more robust the graph.\nThis can be viewed through the lens of network connectivity i.e., larger average inverse distance\n--> better connected graph --> more robust graph .\n\nResolves the issue of not working for disconnected graphs in the avg_distance() function.\n\n:param graph: undirected NetworkX graph\n:return: a float"},{"title":"graph_tiger.measures.diameter(graph, **kwargs)","url":"api-measures.html#api-measures-diameter","text":"The diameter of a connected graph is the longest shortest path between all pairs of nodes.\nThe smaller the diameter the more robust the graph i.e., smaller diameter --> \nbetter connected graph --> more robust graph .\n\n:param graph: undirected NetworkX graph\n:return: an integer"},{"title":"graph_tiger.measures.avg_vertex_betweenness(graph, k=np.inf, **kwargs)","url":"api-measures.html#api-measures-avg_vertex_betweenness","text":"The average vertex betweenness of a graph is the summation of vertex betweenness for every node in the graph.\nThe smaller the average vertex betweenness, the more robust the graph. \nWe can view this as the load of the network being better distributed and\nless dependent on a few nodes .\n\n:param graph: undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: the number of nodes used to approximate betweenness centrality (k=10% of nodes is usually good)\n:return: a float"},{"title":"graph_tiger.measures.avg_edge_betweenness(graph, k=np.inf, **kwargs)","url":"api-measures.html#api-measures-avg_edge_betweenness","text":"Similar to vertex betweenness, edge betweenness is defined as the number of shortest paths\nthat pass through an edge *e* out of the total possible shortest paths.\nThe smaller the average edge betweenness, the more robust the graph. We can view this as the\nload of the network being better distributed and less dependent on a few edges .\n\n:param graph: undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: the number of nodes used to approximate betweenness centrality (k=10% of nodes is usually good)\n:return: a float"},{"title":"graph_tiger.measures.average_clustering_coefficient(graph, **kwargs)","url":"api-measures.html#api-measures-average_clustering_coefficient","text":"The global clustering coefficient is based on the number of triplets of nodes in the graph,\nand provides an indication of how well nodes tend to cluster together.\nThe larger the average global clustering coefficient, the more robust the graph i.e., more triangles -->\nbetter connected --> more robust graph .\n\n:param graph: undirected NetworkX graph\n:return: a float"},{"title":"graph_tiger.measures.largest_connected_component(graph, **kwargs)","url":"api-measures.html#api-measures-largest_connected_component","text":"This measure provides an indication of a graph's connectivity by measuring the number\nof nodes contained in the largest connected component. The larger the value, the more robust the graph.\n\n:param graph: undirected NetworkX graph\n:return: an integer"},{"title":"graph_tiger.measures.spectral_radius(graph, use_gpu=False, **kwargs)","url":"api-measures.html#api-measures-spectral_radius","text":"The largest eigenvalue λ_1 of an adjacency matrix **A** is called the spectral radius.\nThe larger the spectral radius, the more robust the graph. This can be viewed from its close relationship to the\n\"path\" or \"loop\" capacity in a network .\n\n:param graph: undirected NetworkX graph\n:param use_gpu: defaults to False; set to True to use GPU (if available)\n:return: a float"},{"title":"graph_tiger.measures.spectral_gap(graph, use_gpu=False, **kwargs)","url":"api-measures.html#api-measures-spectral_gap","text":"The difference between the largest and second largest eigenvalues of the adjacency matrix\n(λ_1 - λ_2) is called the spectral gap λ_d.\nThe larger the spectral gap, the more robust the graph.\nHas an advantage over spectral radius since it accounts for\nundesirable bridges in the network .\n\n:param graph: undirected NetworkX graph\n:param use_gpu: defaults to False; set to True to use GPU (if available)\n:return: a float"},{"title":"graph_tiger.measures.natural_connectivity(graph, k=np.inf, use_gpu=False, **kwargs)","url":"api-measures.html#api-measures-natural_connectivity","text":"Natural connectivity has a physical and structural interpretation that is tied to the connectivity properties\nof a network, identifying alternative pathways in a network through the weighted number of closed walks.\nThe larger the natural connectivity (average eigenvalue of adjacency matrix), the more robust the graph .\n\n:param graph: undirected NetworkX graph\n:param use_gpu: defaults to False; set to True to use GPU (if available)\n:return: a float"},{"title":"graph_tiger.measures.odd_subgraph_centrality(i, lam, u)","url":"api-measures.html#api-measures-odd_subgraph_centrality","text":"Calculates the number of odd length closed walks that a node participates in .\nUsed in the calculation of spectral scaling and generalized robustness index.\n\n:param i: node index\n:param lam: largest eigenvalue\n:param u: largest eigenvector\n:return: a float"},{"title":"graph_tiger.measures.spectral_scaling(graph, k=np.inf, use_gpu=False, **kwargs)","url":"api-measures.html#api-measures-spectral_scaling","text":"Spectral scaling is a combination of the spectral gap and subgraph centrality. Spectral scaling takes into account\nif a graph has many bridges. The smaller the value, the more robust the graph .\n\n:param graph: undirected NetworkX graph\n:param use_gpu: defaults to False; set to True to use GPU (if available)\n:return: a float"},{"title":"graph_tiger.measures.generalized_robustness_index(graph, k=30, use_gpu=False, **kwargs)","url":"api-measures.html#api-measures-generalized_robustness_index","text":"This can be considered a fast approximation of spectral scaling. The smaller the value, the more robust the graph.\nAlso helps determine if a graph has many bridges (bad for robustness) .\n\n:param graph: undirected NetworkX graph\n:param use_gpu: defaults to False; set to True to use GPU (if available)\n:return: a float"},{"title":"graph_tiger.measures.algebraic_connectivity(graph, **kwargs)","url":"api-measures.html#api-measures-algebraic_connectivity","text":"The larger the algebraic connectivity, the more robust the graph.\nThis is due to it's close connection to edge connectivity, where it serves as a lower bound:\n0 < u_2 < node connectivity < edge connectivity. This means that a network with larger algebraic connectivity\nis harder to disconnect .\n\n:param graph: undirected NetworkX graph\n:return: a float"},{"title":"graph_tiger.measures.num_spanning_trees(graph, k=np.inf, **kwargs)","url":"api-measures.html#api-measures-num_spanning_trees","text":"The number of spanning trees *T* is the number of unique spanning trees that can be found in a graph.\nThe larger the number of spanning trees, the more robust the graph.\nThis can be viewed from the perspective of network connectivity, where a larger set of\nspanning trees means more alternative pathways in the network .\n\n:param graph: undirected NetworkX graph\n:return: a float"},{"title":"graph_tiger.measures.effective_resistance(graph, k=np.inf, **kwargs)","url":"api-measures.html#api-measures-effective_resistance","text":"This measure views a graph as an electrical circuit where an edge (i, j)\ncorresponds to a resister of r_{ij} = 1 Ohm and a node *i* corresponds to a junction.\nWe say the *effective graph resistance* *R* is the sum of resistances for all distinct pairs of vertices\nThe smaller the effective resistance, the more robust the graph .\n\n:param graph: undirected NetworkX graph\n:return: a float"},{"title":"graph_tiger.attacks.run_attack_method(graph, method, k=3, approx=None, seed=None)","url":"api-attacks.html#api-attacks-run_attack_method","text":"Runs a specified attack on an undirected graph, returning a list of nodes or edges.\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param method: a string representing one of the attack methods\n:param k: number of nodes or edges to attack\n:param approx: attack approximation parameter (not available for every measure)\n:param seed: sets the seed in order to obtain reproducible attacks\n:return: a list of nodes or edges selected for attack"},{"title":"graph_tiger.attacks.get_attack_methods()","url":"api-attacks.html#api-attacks-get_attack_methods","text":"Gets a list of available attach methods as a list of functions\n\n:return: a list of all attack functions"},{"title":"graph_tiger.attacks.get_attack_category(method)","url":"api-attacks.html#api-attacks-get_attack_category","text":"Gets the attack category e.g., 'node', 'edge' attack\n\n:param method: a string representing the attack method\n\n:return: a string representing the attack type ('node' or 'edge')"},{"title":"graph_tiger.attacks.get_node_ns(graph, k=3)","url":"api-attacks.html#api-attacks-get_node_ns","text":"Get k nodes to attack based on the NetShield algorithm .\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of nodes to attack\n:return: a list of node labels to attack"},{"title":"graph_tiger.attacks.get_node_pr(graph, k=3)","url":"api-attacks.html#api-attacks-get_node_pr","text":"Get k nodes to attack based on top PageRank entries :citepage1999pagerank.\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of nodes to attack\n\n:return: a list of nodes to attack"},{"title":"graph_tiger.attacks.get_node_eig(graph, k=3)","url":"api-attacks.html#api-attacks-get_node_eig","text":"Get k nodes to attack based on top eigenvector centrality entries\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of nodes to attack\n:return: a list of nodes to attack"},{"title":"graph_tiger.attacks.get_node_id(graph, k=3)","url":"api-attacks.html#api-attacks-get_node_id","text":"Get k nodes to attack based on Initial Degree (ID) Removal .\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of nodes to attack\n\n:return: a list of nodes to attack"},{"title":"graph_tiger.attacks.get_node_rd(graph, k=3)","url":"api-attacks.html#api-attacks-get_node_rd","text":"Get k nodes to attack based on Recalculated Degree (RD) Removal .\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of nodes to attack\n\n:return: a list of nodes to attack"},{"title":"graph_tiger.attacks.get_node_ib(graph, k=3, approx=np.inf, seed=None)","url":"api-attacks.html#api-attacks-get_node_ib","text":"Get k nodes to attack based on Initial Betweenness (IB) Removal .\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of nodes to attack\n:param approx: number of nodes to approximate the betweenness centrality, k=0.1n is a good approximation, where n\n    is the number of nodes in the graph\n\n:return: a list of nodes to attack"},{"title":"graph_tiger.attacks.get_node_rb(graph, k=3, approx=np.inf, seed=None)","url":"api-attacks.html#api-attacks-get_node_rb","text":"Get k nodes to attack based on Recalculated Betweenness (RB) Removal .\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of nodes to attack\n:param approx: number of nodes to approximate the betweenness centrality, k=0.1n is a good approximation, where n\n    is the number of nodes in the graph\n\n:return: a list of nodes to attack"},{"title":"graph_tiger.attacks.get_node_rnd(graph, k=3, rng=None)","url":"api-attacks.html#api-attacks-get_node_rnd","text":"Randomly select k distinct nodes to attack\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of nodes to attack\n\n:return: a list of nodes to attack"},{"title":"graph_tiger.attacks.get_edge_line_ns(graph, k=3)","url":"api-attacks.html#api-attacks-get_edge_line_ns","text":"Get k edges to attack using Netshield by transforming the graph into a line graph \n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of edges to attack\n\n:return: a list of edge tuples to attack"},{"title":"graph_tiger.attacks.get_edge_line_pr(graph, k=3)","url":"api-attacks.html#api-attacks-get_edge_line_pr","text":"Get k edges to attack using PageRank by transforming the graph into a line graph .\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of edges to attack\n\n:return: a list of edge tuples to attack"},{"title":"graph_tiger.attacks.get_edge_line_eig(graph, k=3)","url":"api-attacks.html#api-attacks-get_edge_line_eig","text":"Get k edges to attack using eigenvector centrality by transforming the graph into a line graph .\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of edges to attack\n\n:return: a list of edge tuples to attack"},{"title":"graph_tiger.attacks.get_edge_line_deg(graph, k=3)","url":"api-attacks.html#api-attacks-get_edge_line_deg","text":"Get k edges to attack using degree centrality by transforming the graph into a line graph .\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of edges to attack\n\n:return: a list of edge tuples to attack"},{"title":"graph_tiger.attacks.get_edge_id(graph, k=3)","url":"api-attacks.html#api-attacks-get_edge_id","text":"Get k edges to attack based on Initial Degree (ID) Removal .\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of edges to attack\n\n:return: a list of edge tuples to attack"},{"title":"graph_tiger.attacks.get_edge_rd(graph, k=3)","url":"api-attacks.html#api-attacks-get_edge_rd","text":"Get k edges to attack based on Recalculated Degree (RD) Removal .\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of edges to attack\n\n:return: a list of edge tuples to attack"},{"title":"graph_tiger.attacks.get_edge_ib(graph, k=3, approx=np.inf, seed=None)","url":"api-attacks.html#api-attacks-get_edge_ib","text":"Get k edges to attack based on Initial Betweenness (IB) Removal .\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of edges to attack\n:param approx: number of edges to approximate the betweenness centrality\n\n:return: a list of edge tuples to attack"},{"title":"graph_tiger.attacks.get_edge_rb(graph, k=3, approx=np.inf, seed=None)","url":"api-attacks.html#api-attacks-get_edge_rb","text":"Get k edges to attack based on Recalculated Betweenness (RB) Removal .\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of edges to attack\n:param approx: number of edges to approximate the betweenness centrality\n\n:return: a list of edge tuples to attack"},{"title":"graph_tiger.attacks.get_edge_rnd(graph, k=3, rng=None)","url":"api-attacks.html#api-attacks-get_edge_rnd","text":"Randomly select k edges to attack\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of edges to attack\n\n:return: a list of edge tuples to attack"},{"title":"graph_tiger.attacks.Attack(graph, runs=10, steps=50, attack='id_node', defense=None, k_d=0, **kwargs)","url":"api-attacks.html#api-attacks-Attack","text":"This class simulates a variety of attack strategies on an undirected NetworkX graph\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param runs: an integer number of times to run the simulation\n:param steps: an integer number of steps to run a single simulation\n:param attack: a string representing the attack strategy to run\n:param defense: a string representing the defense strategy to run\n:param k_d: an integer number of nodes to defend\n:param kwargs: see parent class Simulation for additional options"},{"title":"graph_tiger.attacks.Attack.reset_simulation()","url":"api-attacks.html#api-attacks-Attack-reset_simulation","text":"Resets the simulation between each run"},{"title":"graph_tiger.attacks.Attack.track_simulation(step)","url":"api-attacks.html#api-attacks-Attack-track_simulation","text":"Keeps track of important simulation information at each step of the simulation\n\n:param step: current simulation iteration"},{"title":"graph_tiger.attacks.Attack.run_single_sim()","url":"api-attacks.html#api-attacks-Attack-run_single_sim","text":"Run the attack simulation"},{"title":"graph_tiger.defenses.run_defense_method(graph, method, k=3, seed=None)","url":"api-defenses.html#api-defenses-run_defense_method","text":"Runs a specified defense on an undirected graph.\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param method: a string representing one of the defense methods\n:param k: number of nodes or edges to defend\n:param seed: sets the seed in order to obtain reproducible defense runs\n:return: a list of nodes or a dictionary of edge changes"},{"title":"graph_tiger.defenses.get_defense_methods()","url":"api-defenses.html#api-defenses-get_defense_methods","text":"Gets a list of available defense methods as a list of functions.\n\n:return: a list of all defense functions"},{"title":"graph_tiger.defenses.get_defense_category(method)","url":"api-defenses.html#api-defenses-get_defense_category","text":"Gets the defense category e.g., 'node', 'edge' defense.\n\n:param method: a string representing the defense method\n:return: a string representing the defense type ('node' or 'edge')"},{"title":"graph_tiger.defenses.get_node_ns(graph, k=3)","url":"api-defenses.html#api-defenses-get_node_ns","text":"Get k nodes to defend based on the Netshield algorithm .\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of nodes to defend\n\n:return: a list of nodes to defend"},{"title":"graph_tiger.defenses.get_node_pr(graph, k=3)","url":"api-defenses.html#api-defenses-get_node_pr","text":"Get k nodes to defend based on top PageRank entries .\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of nodes to defend\n\n:return: a list of nodes to defend"},{"title":"graph_tiger.defenses.get_node_eig(graph, k=3)","url":"api-defenses.html#api-defenses-get_node_eig","text":"Get k nodes to defend based on top eigenvector centrality entries\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of nodes to defend\n:return: a list of nodes to defend"},{"title":"graph_tiger.defenses.get_node_ib(graph, k=3, approx=np.inf)","url":"api-defenses.html#api-defenses-get_node_ib","text":"Get k nodes to defend based on Initial Betweenness (IB) Removal .\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of nodes to defend\n:param approx: number of nodes to approximate the betweenness centrality, k=0.1n is a good approximation, where n\n    is the number of nodes in the graph\n\n:return: a list of nodes to defend"},{"title":"graph_tiger.defenses.get_node_rb(graph, k=3, approx=np.inf)","url":"api-defenses.html#api-defenses-get_node_rb","text":"Get k nodes to defend based on Recalculated Betweenness (RB) Removal \n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of nodes to defend\n:param approx: number of nodes to approximate the betweenness centrality, k=0.1n is a good approximation, where n\n    is the number of nodes in the graph\n\n:return: a list of nodes to defend"},{"title":"graph_tiger.defenses.get_node_id(graph, k=3)","url":"api-defenses.html#api-defenses-get_node_id","text":"Get k nodes to defend based on Initial Degree (ID) Removal .\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of nodes to defend\n\n:return: a list of nodes to defend"},{"title":"graph_tiger.defenses.get_node_rd(graph, k=3)","url":"api-defenses.html#api-defenses-get_node_rd","text":"Get k nodes to defend based on Recalculated Degree (RD) Removal .\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of nodes to defend\n\n:return: a list of nodes to defend"},{"title":"graph_tiger.defenses.get_node_rnd(graph, k=3, rng=None)","url":"api-defenses.html#api-defenses-get_node_rnd","text":"Randomly select k distinct nodes to defend\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of nodes to defend\n\n:return: a list of nodes to defend"},{"title":"graph_tiger.defenses.get_central_edges(graph, k, method='eig')","url":"api-defenses.html#api-defenses-get_central_edges","text":"Internal function to compute edge PageRank, eigenvector centrality and degree centrality.\n\n:param graph: undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: int number of edges to add\n:param method: string representing defense method\n:return: list of edges to add"},{"title":"graph_tiger.defenses.add_edge_pr(graph, k=3)","url":"api-defenses.html#api-defenses-add_edge_pr","text":"Get k edges to defend based on top edge PageRank entries .\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of edges to add\n:return: a dictionary of the edges to be 'added'"},{"title":"graph_tiger.defenses.add_edge_eig(graph, k=3)","url":"api-defenses.html#api-defenses-add_edge_eig","text":"Get k edges to defend based on top edge eigenvector centrality entries .\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of edges to add\n:return: a dictionary of the edges to be 'added'"},{"title":"graph_tiger.defenses.add_edge_degree(graph, k=3)","url":"api-defenses.html#api-defenses-add_edge_degree","text":"Add k edges to defend based on top edge degree centrality entries .\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of edges to add\n:return: a dictionary of the edges to be 'added'"},{"title":"graph_tiger.defenses.add_edge_rnd(graph, k=3, rng=None)","url":"api-defenses.html#api-defenses-add_edge_rnd","text":"Add k random nonedges to the graph.\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of edges to add\n:param rng: optional NumPy random generator\n:return: a dictionary of the edges to be 'added'"},{"title":"graph_tiger.defenses.add_edge_pref(graph, k=3)","url":"api-defenses.html#api-defenses-add_edge_pref","text":"Adds edges between the lowest-degree feasible pairs .\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of edges to add\n:return: a dictionary of the edges to be 'added'"},{"title":"graph_tiger.defenses.get_random_nonedges(graph, k, rng, excluded=None)","url":"api-defenses.html#api-defenses-get_random_nonedges","text":"Return k random nonedges, excluding specified unordered node pairs."},{"title":"graph_tiger.defenses.rewire_edge_rnd(graph, k=3, rng=None)","url":"api-defenses.html#api-defenses-rewire_edge_rnd","text":"Removes k random edges and adds k different random nonedges .\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of edges to rewire\n:param rng: optional NumPy random generator\n:return: a dictionary of the edges to be 'removed' and edges to be 'added'"},{"title":"graph_tiger.defenses.rewire_edge_rnd_neighbor(graph, k=3, rng=None)","url":"api-defenses.html#api-defenses-rewire_edge_rnd_neighbor","text":"Randomly removes a neighbor edge and adds a different random edge .\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of edges to rewire\n:param rng: optional NumPy random generator\n:return: a dictionary of the edges to be 'removed' and edges to be 'added'"},{"title":"graph_tiger.defenses.rewire_edge_pref(graph, k=3, rng=None)","url":"api-defenses.html#api-defenses-rewire_edge_pref","text":"Detaches a neighbor of a highest-degree node and reconnects that neighbor elsewhere.\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of edges to rewire\n:param rng: optional NumPy random generator\n:return: a dictionary of the edges to be 'removed' and edges to be 'added'"},{"title":"graph_tiger.defenses.rewire_edge_pref_rnd(graph, k=3, rng=None)","url":"api-defenses.html#api-defenses-rewire_edge_pref_rnd","text":"Disconnects the higher-degree endpoint and reconnects the other endpoint randomly.\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of edges to rewire\n:param rng: optional NumPy random generator\n:return: a dictionary of the edges to be 'removed' and edges to be 'added'"},{"title":"graph_tiger.defenses.Defense(graph, runs=10, steps=50, attack='id_node', defense=None, k_d=0, **kwargs)","url":"api-defenses.html#api-defenses-Defense","text":"This class simulates a variety of defense techniques on an undirected NetworkX graph\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param runs: an integer number of times to run the simulation\n:param steps: an integer number of steps to run a single simulation\n:param attack: a string representing the attack strategy to run\n:param defense: a string representing the defense strategy to run\n:param k_d: an integer number of nodes to defend\n:param kwargs: see parent class Simulation for additional options"},{"title":"graph_tiger.defenses.Defense.reset_simulation()","url":"api-defenses.html#api-defenses-Defense-reset_simulation","text":"Resets the simulation between each run"},{"title":"graph_tiger.defenses.Defense.track_simulation(step)","url":"api-defenses.html#api-defenses-Defense-track_simulation","text":"Keeps track of important simulation information at each step of the simulation\n\n:param step: current simulation iteration"},{"title":"graph_tiger.defenses.Defense.run_single_sim()","url":"api-defenses.html#api-defenses-Defense-run_single_sim","text":"Run the defense simulation"},{"title":"graph_tiger.simulations.Simulation(graph, runs, steps, **kwargs)","url":"api-simulations.html#api-simulations-Simulation","text":"The parent class for all simulation classes i.e., attack, defense, cascading failure and diffusion models.\nProvides a shared set of functions, largely for network visualization and plotting of results\n\n:param graph: undirected NetworkX graph\n:param runs: number of times to run the simulation\n:param steps: number of time steps to run each simulation\n:param kwargs: optional parameters to change visualization settings"},{"title":"graph_tiger.simulations.Simulation.begin_reset()","url":"api-simulations.html#api-simulations-Simulation-begin_reset","text":"Start the next reproducible simulation run."},{"title":"graph_tiger.simulations.Simulation.get_random_seed()","url":"api-simulations.html#api-simulations-Simulation-get_random_seed","text":"Return the next reproducible seed for a child operation."},{"title":"graph_tiger.simulations.Simulation.child_class()","url":"api-simulations.html#api-simulations-Simulation-child_class","text":"Gets the child class name\n:return: string"},{"title":"graph_tiger.simulations.Simulation.get_graph_coordinates()","url":"api-simulations.html#api-simulations-Simulation-get_graph_coordinates","text":"Gets the graph coordinates, which can be:\n(1) set in the graph itself with the 'pos' tag on the vertices,\n(2) positioned according to the force atlas2 algorithm,\n(3) positioned using a spectral layout.\n\nThen lays out the edges, can be curved, bundled, or straight\n\n:return: Tuple containing node and edge positions"},{"title":"graph_tiger.simulations.Simulation.plot_results(results)","url":"api-simulations.html#api-simulations-Simulation-plot_results","text":"Plots the compiled simulation results\n\n:param results: a list of floats representing each simulation output"},{"title":"graph_tiger.simulations.Simulation.get_plot_title(step)","url":"api-simulations.html#api-simulations-Simulation-get_plot_title","text":"Gets the title for each plot\n\n:param step: the current simulation iteration\n:return: title string"},{"title":"graph_tiger.simulations.Simulation.plot_graph_transition(sim_info)","url":"api-simulations.html#api-simulations-Simulation-plot_graph_transition","text":"Helper function to decide which snapshots to take for network visualization\n\n:param sim_info: the information stored at each step in the simulation"},{"title":"graph_tiger.simulations.Simulation.get_visual_settings(step)","url":"api-simulations.html#api-simulations-Simulation-get_visual_settings","text":"Sets the visual settings for the network visualization\n\n:param step: current iteration of the simulation\n:return: four lists, each containing a number corresponding to the size or color of each node in the graph + cmap representing color scheme"},{"title":"graph_tiger.simulations.Simulation.draw_graph(step)","url":"api-simulations.html#api-simulations-Simulation-draw_graph","text":"Draws the graph\n\n:param step: current iteration of the simulation\n:return: matplotlib.collections.PathCollection PathCollection of the nodes."},{"title":"graph_tiger.simulations.Simulation.plot_network(step)","url":"api-simulations.html#api-simulations-Simulation-plot_network","text":"Plots the compiled simulation results\n\n:param step: current iteration of the simulation"},{"title":"graph_tiger.simulations.Simulation.create_simulation_gif()","url":"api-simulations.html#api-simulations-Simulation-create_simulation_gif","text":"Draws and saves the network simulation to an MP4 file"},{"title":"graph_tiger.simulations.Simulation.run_simulation()","url":"api-simulations.html#api-simulations-Simulation-run_simulation","text":"Averages the simulation over the number of 'runs'.\n\n:return: a list containing the average value at each 'step' of the simulation."},{"title":"graph_tiger.simulations.Simulation.reset_simulation()","url":"api-simulations.html#api-simulations-Simulation-reset_simulation","text":"Implemented by child class"},{"title":"graph_tiger.simulations.Simulation.run_single_sim()","url":"api-simulations.html#api-simulations-Simulation-run_single_sim","text":"Implemented by child class"},{"title":"graph_tiger.simulations.Simulation.get_effective_strength()","url":"api-simulations.html#api-simulations-Simulation-get_effective_strength","text":"Implemented by child class"},{"title":"graph_tiger.cascading.Cascading(graph, model='motter_lai', runs=10, steps=100, l=0.8, r=0.2, beta=0, allocation='degree', initial_load=None, capacities=None, initial_failures=None, **kwargs)","url":"api-cascading.html#api-cascading-Cascading","text":"This class simulates cascading failures on a network.\n\nThe default Motter-Lai model uses unnormalized shortest-path betweenness as\nnode load and fixes capacity at (1 + r) times the initial load. After an\nattack, overloaded nodes fail synchronously .\n\nIn the Crucitti model, 1 + r is the paper's tolerance parameter. An\noverloaded node remains in the network while the efficiencies of its incident\nedges decrease by its capacity-to-load ratio. Traffic is synchronously rerouted\nover the most efficient weighted paths and results report average network\nefficiency .\n\nThe local-load-sharing model gives node i initial load equal to its degree\nand fixed capacity (1 + r) L_i(0). A failed node redistributes its complete\ncurrent load among functioning neighbors with weights proportional to\ndegree ** beta. Thus beta=0 gives equal sharing, while larger values\nincreasingly favor high-degree recipients .\nAlternatively, allocation selects greedy or proportional spare-capacity\nsharing, or coordinated maximum flow. Every policy transfers the full load\nwhen a recipient exists. Only work with no recipient becomes lost service.\n\nThe historical TIGER redistribution rule remains available as\nmodel='legacy_redistribution'. It is a TIGER-specific model.\n\n:param graph: an undirected NetworkX graph\n:param model: cascading model (motter_lai, crucitti, local_load_sharing,\n    or legacy_redistribution)\n:param runs: an integer number of times to run the simulation\n:param steps: an integer number of steps to run a single simulation\n:param l: a float representing the maximum initial load in the legacy model\n:param r: a float representing the amount of redundancy in the network\n:param beta: a nonnegative degree-preference exponent for local load sharing\n:param allocation: local policy: degree (default), greedy, proportional, or\n    max_flow. Greedy ties follow graph node insertion order. Maximum flow\n    uses Edmonds-Karp with that order; ties can affect subsequent failures.\n:param initial_load: optional complete node-to-load mapping for local sharing;\n    finite nonnegative values, default intact unweighted degrees\n:param capacities: optional complete node-to-capacity mapping for local sharing;\n    finite nonnegative values, default (1+r) times initial load. Explicit\n    capacities are used directly, without another redundancy multiplier.\n:param initial_failures: optional iterable of initially failed nodes for local\n    sharing; replaces attack selection and its budget, including when empty\n:param kwargs: see parent class Simulation for additional options"},{"title":"graph_tiger.cascading.Cascading.validate_parameters()","url":"api-cascading.html#api-cascading-Cascading-validate_parameters","text":"Validate cascading-model parameters."},{"title":"graph_tiger.cascading.Cascading.get_load(graph, weight=None)","url":"api-cascading.html#api-cascading-Cascading-get_load","text":"Compute unnormalized shortest-path node load.\n\n:param graph: functioning NetworkX graph\n:param weight: optional edge-distance attribute used for weighted routing\n:return: dictionary mapping node labels to load"},{"title":"graph_tiger.cascading.Cascading.shed_load","url":"api-cascading.html#api-cascading-Cascading-shed_load","text":"Backward-compatible alias for lost_load; no deliberate shedding."},{"title":"graph_tiger.cascading.Cascading.get_efficiency_graph(graph)","url":"api-cascading.html#api-cascading-Cascading-get_efficiency_graph","text":"Convert edge efficiencies into positive routing distances.\n\nZero-efficiency edges remain in the simulation graph but are unavailable\nto the most-efficient-path calculation.\n\n:param graph: current Crucitti simulation graph\n:return: graph copy with reciprocal edge distances"},{"title":"graph_tiger.cascading.Cascading.get_efficiency(graph)","url":"api-cascading.html#api-cascading-Cascading-get_efficiency","text":"Compute average weighted network efficiency from the Crucitti model.\n\nPath efficiency is the reciprocal of the sum of reciprocal edge\nefficiencies, matching the harmonic path composition in\n.\n\n:param graph: current functioning graph\n:return: average efficiency over ordered node pairs"},{"title":"graph_tiger.cascading.Cascading.reset_simulation()","url":"api-cascading.html#api-cascading-Cascading-reset_simulation","text":"Resets the simulation between each run."},{"title":"graph_tiger.cascading.Cascading.track_simulation(step)","url":"api-cascading.html#api-cascading-Cascading-track_simulation","text":"Keeps track of important simulation information at each step of the simulation.\n\n:param step: current simulation iteration"},{"title":"graph_tiger.cascading.Cascading.run_motter_lai_step()","url":"api-cascading.html#api-cascading-Cascading-run_motter_lai_step","text":"Recompute load and synchronously fail overloaded functioning nodes.\n\n:return: set of nodes that fail in this step"},{"title":"graph_tiger.cascading.Cascading.run_crucitti_step()","url":"api-cascading.html#api-cascading-Cascading-run_crucitti_step","text":"Update incident edge efficiencies without removing overloaded nodes.\n\nAll edge updates use the loads at the beginning of the step. If both\nendpoints are overloaded, the lower capacity-to-load ratio determines\nthe undirected edge efficiency.\n\n:return: whether any edge efficiency changed"},{"title":"graph_tiger.cascading.Cascading.run_local_load_sharing_step()","url":"api-cascading.html#api-cascading-Cascading-run_local_load_sharing_step","text":"Transfer complete failed workloads, then synchronously fail overloads.\n\nDegree weights use the intact graph. Greedy and proportional allocations\nuse pre-round headroom independently for each source. Maximum flow\ncoordinates the capacity-fitting pass across all sources. Greedy and\nmaximum-flow leftovers are divided equally among eligible recipients;\nproportional sharing uses equal shares when all headroom is zero.\nlast_transfers maps (failed source, recipient) to the completed transfer.\nlost_load accumulates work with no functioning neighbor; shed_load is\nretained as a compatibility alias, not a deliberate shedding control.\n\n:return: set of nodes that fail in this step"},{"title":"graph_tiger.cascading.Cascading.run_legacy_step()","url":"api-cascading.html#api-cascading-Cascading-run_legacy_step","text":"Redistribute each newly failed node's load once among functioning neighbors.\n\n:return: set of nodes that fail in this step"},{"title":"graph_tiger.cascading.Cascading.run_single_sim()","url":"api-cascading.html#api-cascading-Cascading-run_single_sim","text":"Run the cascading-failure simulation."},{"title":"graph_tiger.diffusion.Diffusion(graph, model='SIS', runs=10, steps=5000, b=0.00208, d=0.01, c=1, **kwargs)","url":"api-diffusion.html#api-diffusion-Diffusion","text":"Simulates the propagation of a virus using either the SIS or SIR model .\n\n:param graph: contact network\n:param model: a string to set the model type (i.e., SIS or SIR)\n:param runs: an integer number of times to run the simulation\n:param steps: an integer number of steps to run a single simulation\n:param b: float representing birth rate of virus (probability of transmitting disease to each neighbor)\n:param d: float representing death rate of virus (probability of each infected node healing)\n:param c: fraction of initially infected nodes\n:param kwargs: see parent class Simulation for additional options"},{"title":"graph_tiger.diffusion.Diffusion.get_effective_strength()","url":"api-diffusion.html#api-diffusion-Diffusion-get_effective_strength","text":"Gets the effective string of the virus. This is a factor of the spectral radius (first eigenvalue) of graph,\nthe virus birth rate 'b' and the virus death rate 'd'\n\n:return: a float for virus effective strength"},{"title":"graph_tiger.diffusion.Diffusion.reset_simulation()","url":"api-diffusion.html#api-diffusion-Diffusion-reset_simulation","text":"Resets the simulation between each run"},{"title":"graph_tiger.diffusion.Diffusion.validate_parameters()","url":"api-diffusion.html#api-diffusion-Diffusion-validate_parameters","text":"Validate discrete-time SIS/SIR parameters."},{"title":"graph_tiger.diffusion.Diffusion.track_simulation(step)","url":"api-diffusion.html#api-diffusion-Diffusion-track_simulation","text":"Keeps track of important simulation information at each step of the simulation\n\n:param step: current simulation iteration"},{"title":"graph_tiger.diffusion.Diffusion.run_single_sim()","url":"api-diffusion.html#api-diffusion-Diffusion-run_single_sim","text":"The initially infected nodes are chosen uniformly at random. At each time step,\nevery infected-susceptible edge transmits independently with probability 'b'.\nEvery node infected at the start of the step recovers with probability 'd' and\nbecomes susceptible again in SIS or permanently recovered in SIR."},{"title":"graph_tiger.influence.Influence(graph, model='independent_cascade', runs=10, steps=100, seeds=None, probability=0.1, weight='weight', threshold='threshold', initial_state=None, message_seeds=None, tracked_state=1, tie_break='random', priority=None, **kwargs)","url":"api-influence.html#api-influence-Influence","text":"Simulate progressive or reversible influence on a NetworkX graph.\n\nIndependent-cascade and linear-threshold conventions follow .\nThe voter model follows ; the competitive process is\nbased on  with an explicit same-round tie rule.\n\n:param graph: simple NetworkX graph or directed graph\n:param model: independent_cascade, linear_threshold, voter, or competitive_cascade\n:param runs: number of simulation realizations\n:param steps: number of synchronous rounds or asynchronous voter updates\n:param seeds: initially active nodes for independent cascade or linear threshold\n:param probability: scalar probability, edge-attribute name, or message mapping\n:param weight: edge attribute used by linear threshold; missing values default to one\n:param threshold: scalar, node-attribute name, or node-to-threshold mapping\n:param initial_state: complete node-to-state mapping for the voter model\n:param message_seeds: message-to-seed-set mapping for competitive cascade\n:param tracked_state: state or message counted in the returned trajectory\n:param tie_break: random or priority for simultaneous competitive-cascade arrivals\n:param priority: message order used when tie_break is priority\n:param kwargs: see parent class Simulation for random seed and plotting options"},{"title":"graph_tiger.influence.Influence.validate_parameters()","url":"api-influence.html#api-influence-Influence-validate_parameters","text":"Validate graph, model, initial-state, and model-specific parameters."},{"title":"graph_tiger.influence.Influence.reset_simulation()","url":"api-influence.html#api-influence-Influence-reset_simulation","text":"Reset graph state and advance to the next reproducible random stream."},{"title":"graph_tiger.influence.Influence.run_independent_cascade_step()","url":"api-influence.html#api-influence-Influence-run_independent_cascade_step","text":"Advance one synchronous independent-cascade frontier.\n\n:return: set of nodes activated in this round"},{"title":"graph_tiger.influence.Influence.run_linear_threshold_step()","url":"api-influence.html#api-influence-Influence-run_linear_threshold_step","text":"Advance one synchronous linear-threshold frontier.\n\n:return: set of nodes activated in this round"},{"title":"graph_tiger.influence.Influence.run_voter_step()","url":"api-influence.html#api-influence-Influence-run_voter_step","text":"Perform one asynchronous node-copying event.\n\n:return: the changed node as a set, or an empty set"},{"title":"graph_tiger.influence.Influence.run_competitive_cascade_step()","url":"api-influence.html#api-influence-Influence-run_competitive_cascade_step","text":"Advance all competitive-message frontiers by one synchronous round.\n\n:return: set of nodes that received at least one successful proposal"},{"title":"graph_tiger.influence.Influence.track_simulation(step)","url":"api-influence.html#api-influence-Influence-track_simulation","text":"Store node states, counts, changes, and frontiers for one step."},{"title":"graph_tiger.influence.Influence.run_single_sim()","url":"api-influence.html#api-influence-Influence-run_single_sim","text":"Run one realization for the configured number of steps.\n\n:return: active counts for progressive models or tracked-state counts otherwise"},{"title":"graph_tiger.influence.Influence.get_plot_title(step)","url":"api-influence.html#api-influence-Influence-get_plot_title","text":"Return a stable filename stem for an influence plot."},{"title":"graph_tiger.influence.Influence.plot_results(results)","url":"api-influence.html#api-influence-Influence-plot_results","text":"Plot a normalized active-state or tracked-state trajectory."},{"title":"graph_tiger.influence.Influence.get_visual_settings(step)","url":"api-influence.html#api-influence-Influence-get_visual_settings","text":"Return node and edge styles for a stored influence state."},{"title":"graph_tiger.influence.Influence.plot_graph_transition(sim_info)","url":"api-influence.html#api-influence-Influence-plot_graph_transition","text":"Save representative snapshots from one influence realization."},{"title":"graph_tiger.utils.gpu_available()","url":"api-utils.html#api-utils-gpu_available","text":"Return whether CuPy is importable without relying on pip internals."},{"title":"graph_tiger.utils.get_sparse_graph(graph)","url":"api-utils.html#api-utils-get_sparse_graph","text":"Returns a sparse adjacency matrix in CSR format\n\n:param graph: undirected NetworkX graph\n:return: Scipy sparse adjacency matrix"},{"title":"graph_tiger.utils.get_adjacency_spectrum(graph, k=np.inf, eigvals_only=False, which='LA', use_gpu=False)","url":"api-utils.html#api-utils-get_adjacency_spectrum","text":"Gets the top k eigenpairs of the adjacency matrix\n\nExact spectra are returned when k is infinite or at least the graph order.\n\n:param graph: undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of top k eigenpairs to obtain\n:param eigvals_only: get only the eigenvalues i.e., no eigenvectors\n:param which: the type of k eigenvectors and eigenvalues to find\n:param use_gpu: use CuPy for an explicitly requested partial spectrum\n:return: the eigenpair information"},{"title":"graph_tiger.utils.get_laplacian_spectrum(graph, k=np.inf, which='SM', tol=1e-08, eigvals_only=True, use_gpu=False)","url":"api-utils.html#api-utils-get_laplacian_spectrum","text":"Gets the bottom k eigenpairs of the Laplacian matrix\n\nExact spectra are returned when k is infinite or at least the graph order.\n\n:param graph: undirected NetworkX graph\n:param k: number of bottom k eigenpairs to obtain\n:param which: the type of k eigenvectors and eigenvalues to find\n:param tol: the precision at which to stop computing partial eigenpairs\n:param eigvals_only: get only the eigenvalues i.e., no eigenvectors\n:param use_gpu: retained for API compatibility; Laplacian GPU is unavailable\n:return: the eigenpair information"},{"title":"graph_tiger.utils.get_laplacian(graph)","url":"api-utils.html#api-utils-get_laplacian","text":"Gets the Laplacian matrix in sparse CSR format\n\n:param graph: undirected NetworkX graph\n:return: Scipy sparse Laplacian matrix"}];